\par\par \b \uc2 ECONOMIC SIGNALS-South Korea adds new weapons to arsenal as clouds gather\b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoo Choonsik   \par \uc2 817 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2011\par 09:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Oct 11 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprisingly small \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September were probably based on lessons learnt from past crises, and underscored a policy dilemma in tackling the opposing risks facing one of Asia's most vulnerable economies.\par \par \uc2 As global investors dumped riskier assets in recent weeks, the Bank of Korea sold only $3-4 billion to slow the won's\par \par \uc2 sharp fall, out of the more than $300 billion of reserves it manages, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed last week.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts said they were surprised at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's relative restraint, especially as government officials were calling for calm and reviving weekly crisis meetings to try to stem capital flight.\par \par \uc2 Analysts noted the overall \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was less than what regional rival Taiwan spent defending its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  . The won ended up losing over 9 percent to the dollar in September, far more than the Taiwan unit's 5 percent fall.\par \par \uc2 The following figures and graphics explain the complicated risks facing South Korea's policymakers and Asia's fourth-largest economy, which relies heavily on exports for growth as domestic demand remains depressed:\par \par \uc2 WHY DIDN'T IT SPEND MORE ON \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 The country's foreign reserves stood at a record $312 billion, twice the size of its short-term overseas debt and sufficient to cover some seven months of imports, but defending the highly symbolic $300 billion mark was important politically.\par \par \uc2 Despite fears of massive outflows, the actual amount of foreign funds leaving the country was relatively modest as countries such as China, Thailand and Kazakhstan kept on buying South Korean won bonds to diversify their reserves away from the dollar or euro, offsetting sales of Korean assets by panicky Western investors.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts also said the Bank of Korea had room to short dollar/won positions in derivatives markets to stem the won's decline, limiting the use of reserves money which is disclosed to the public every month.\par \par \uc2 LESSONS FROM CRISES, NEW CAPITAL CONTROLS\par \par \uc2 South Korea saw its foreign reserves plunge by one-fifth or nearly $50 billion in four months in late 2008 as the global financial crisis raged. Policymakers spent most of the funds fighting to prop up the free-falling won, only to witness the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lose over 30 percent of its value against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 That lesson, combined with current-day worries over how long Europe's festering debt crisis will last and how much damage it will inflict on the global economy, likely persuaded South Korea to save as much ammunition as possible in case things get worse.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's heavy short-term overseas debt and easy flows of capital leave it especially vulnerable during times of crisis, undermining the won. But policymakers have implemented a string of more sophisticated measures since 2008/09 aimed at reducing the economy's exposure to external shocks.\par \par \uc2 The country has cut the ratio of short-term debt to foreign reserves to less than 50 percent from nearly 80 percent a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 Since last year, it also has introduced three sets of capital controls, including outright limitations on the amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives trade, and can now adjust these measures if needed to try to control capital flows.\par \par \uc2 INFLATION PAST PEAK, EXPORTS LOSING STEAM\par \par \uc2 To be sure, a weaker won will also help South Korean exporters cope better with shrinking global demand, adding to the argument against massive \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pin the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stronger levels. In 2008-2009, exporters boosted global market share on the back of a 40 percent fall in the won's value over several months.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's exports have held up well when measured in dollar value terms, offsetting sluggish domestic demand, but manufacturing production has been weakening while inventories of finished goods are piling up as global consumption ebbs.\par \par \uc2 The won had risen more than 8 percent against the dollar by mid-summer, helping curb imported inflation and thus giving authorities more leeway in deciding the pace of interest rate increases. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave back all those gains in the late summer market rout, and is now down 2.7 percent in the year to date.\par \par \uc2 Consumer inflation is believed to have peaked in August and eased in September, and a collapse in farm product prices from unusually high levels in 2010 should offset the rise in import prices due to the won's weakening. That would give the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more room to focus on the risks from an economic slowdown and further market turmoil.\par \par \uc2 Both the finance minister and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief admitted in a surprisingly swift manner that this year's inflation target would not be reachable, indicating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy will not be fully skewed toward curbing inflation.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Jonathan Hopfner & Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 KOREA-ECONOMY/ (ECONOMIC SIGNALS)   \pa