\par\par \b \uc2 DJ CORRECT(9/9): INTERVIEW: Japan Trade Official Slams S Korea Efforts To Weaken Won\b0\par\par\uc2 888 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2011\par 05:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 ("INTERVIEW: Japan Trade Official Slams S Korea Efforts To Weaken Won," on Sept. 9, misquoted Hideichi Okada as referring specifically to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 1st and 7th paragraphs. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 -- Japan's top trade official expressed concern over South Korea's efforts to weaken won\par \par \uc2 -- Says cheap won versus yen poses "serious challenge" to Japanese industries\par \par \uc2 -- Says nations must resolutely fight signs of trade, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars\par \par \uc2 -- Says Japan should deal with any sudden yen rises but also consider positive aspects of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -- Expects Japan to make progress on free trade negotiations with EU, China, Korea\par \par \uc2 By Mitsuru Obe and Arran Scott\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's top trade negotiator Friday expressed concern over South Korea's efforts to depress the value of the won, saying that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's relative weakness against the yen posed a "serious challenge" to Japanese industries and indicating that it was inappropriate for Seoul - or indeed any country -- to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market except in extraordinary circumstances.\par \par \uc2 But Hideichi Okada, vice minister for international affairs at the Ministry Of Economy, Trade and Industry, also praised progress by South Korea in crafting free trade agreements and stressed the need for Japan to strike similar deals with its major trading partners.\par \par \uc2 Okada emphasized the need for countries to continue to open up global trade, and played down the risk of a rise in protectionism that some analysts have warned may emerge if the global economy takes a turn for the worse.\par \par \uc2 "Countries still remember that protectionist measures taken after the Great Depression spiraled the global economy into a worldwide depression, so the risk of a similar trade war repeating itself is very small," Okada told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. "But this does not mean we can drop our guard. We have to fight against any signs of trade and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars with strong resolve."\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the senior official expressed frustration with moves by the Bank of Korea to curb a rise in the won. The BOK has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to fight rises in the won, which like other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has faced strong upward pressure as a result of dollar weakness brought on by the U.S. Federal Reserve's easy monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Korea has had much more success in tempering the strength in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than Japan has had in curbing the rise in the yen, which has set post-World War II highs against the dollar despite \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities. The won's relative weakness against the yen has given Korean exporters an edge over their Japanese rivals in export markets like the U.S. and Europe, where Japanese and Korean automakers and electronics companies compete fiercely.\par \par \uc2 "The Korean won has gotten very cheap in relation to the Japanese yen. This has posed a serious challenge to Japanese industries competing against their Korean rivals," Okada said. "It is undesirable for any nation to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the absence of any unavoidable emergency situation."\par \par \uc2 "A country should not intentionally depreciate its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The value of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be decided in the market," he added.\par \par \uc2 Okada pointed out, however, that a relatively weak won did have some drawbacks for Korea, noting that some Korean companies depend on Japan for key components in the production of automobiles and electronics products.\par \par \uc2 With respect to Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, which is set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Okada said "a sudden appreciation of the yen needs to be dealt with."\par \par \uc2 He said the yen is on the strong side, noting that the dollar, which traded at Y77.78 late Friday, was below the Y80 level that the bulk of Japanese companies have been counting on. But he said Japan also needs to take into account the positive aspects of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength, and said he didn't think the yen would return to much lower levels any time soon.\par \par \uc2 "It is necessary to think of the strong yen as a plus for the country," Okada said. For instance, he said, the strong yen could allow Japan to acquire key resources, such as coal and natural gas, helping the nation prepare for an expected decline in the supply of nuclear-generated power in the future, following the disaster at the Fukushima nuclear plant in March.\par \par \uc2 He said he expects Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda to reiterate Japan's intent to pursue free trade agreements with its major trading partners when he makes a policy speech in an extraordinary parliamentary session expected to begin next week. The remark suggests Noda may renew a commitment to join negotiations for a trans-Pacific free trade area involving the U.S. in the near future.\par \par \uc2 This autumn, the leaders of Japan and the European Union are expected to launch formal negotiations for free trade, he said. Also, Japan is proceeding at pace with the aim of launching talks with Korea and China next year aimed at creating a trilateral FTA, he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Mitsuru Obe and Arran Scott, Dow Jones Newswires; +813-6269-2770; mitsuru.obe@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 13, 2011 23:27 ET (03:27 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110914e79e000iq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
