\par\par \b \uc2 S Korea Won Up On Greece Bailout Plan; BOK Likely  \highlight22\uc2 Intervened \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2011\par 11:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     Close     Change \par  USD/KRW            1,051.90  -2.70 \par  JPY/KRW            13.3830   +0.0031 \par  3-Year Treasurys   3.79%     +4 bps \par  5-Year Treasurys   3.98%     +3 bps \par  10-Year Treasurys  4.18%     +1 bp \par  20-Year Treasurys  4.29%     +1 bp \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won rose against the U.S. dollar late Friday as risky assets strengthened broadly after European leaders agreed on a second bailout plan for Greece, but the gains were capped by suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  toward the close of the domestic session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit an intraday low of KRW1,050.00--its lowest level since KRW1,048 on Aug. 22, 2008--as the euro and local stock index extend gains, before cutting losses because of suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed by some stop-loss buying.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea likely bought the dollar in late trading, lifting it from near KRW1,050.00 to KRW1,051.90, according to four Seoul-based traders. Three of those traders estimated the buying totaled more than $300 million.\par \par \uc2 An official at the BOK's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market team declined to comment.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were last suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on July 8, buying the dollar near KRW1,056.\par \par \uc2 "While the won is expected to continue receive upward pressure on solid domestic economic fundamentals, how the Bank of Korea reacts to the pressure will likely decide the pace of its rise," said a dealer at a foreign bank in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 He tipped a KRW1,040-KRW1,060 range for the dollar in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Government bonds and bond futures ended lower amid increased investor risk appetite after European leaders agreed to a rescue plan for debt-laden Greece.\par \par \uc2 "Foreign selling in the futures market also led the overall bond market environment, which is weakening a bit after the euro-zone agreement," said a bond manager at a local asset management firm.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors were net sellers of 12,241 futures contracts after they snapped an eight-day net buying streak Thursday. September bond futures ended 13 ticks lower at 103.15.\par \par \uc2 "Shorter-end bond yields appear to have more room to rise than longer-end ones, considering a possible BOK interest-rate hike in the near future," the manager added.\par \par \uc2 He pegged resistance for the three-year yield at 3.85%.\par \par \uc2 -By Jieun Shin, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-3700-1905; jieun.shin@dowjones.com [ 22-07-11 0926GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1063 1858   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110722e77m000p2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
