\par\par \b \uc2 S Korea Won Up At Near 3-Year High, BOK Likely  \highlight22\uc2 Intervened \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2011\par 10:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     Close    Change \par  USD/KRW            1,057.00 -7.10 \par  JPY/KRW            13.0044  -0.1359 \par  3-Year Treasurys   3.85%    +6 bps \par  5-Year Treasurys   4.08%    +4 bps \par  10-Year Treasurys  4.34%    +3 bps \par  20-Year Treasurys  4.45%    +4 bps \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won Friday rode robust sentiment for Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to its highest level against the dollar in almost three years, which likely triggered won-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar opened weaker on renewed optimism about the pace of the global economic recovery following strong U.S. employment and retail sales data, and it slid as far down as KRW1,056.20--its lowest since marking KRW1,048 on Aug. 22, 2008--on a bout of dollar selling by offshore players.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's descent from there was stalled by suspected dollar buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  near KRW1,056, according to three Seoul-based traders.\par \par \uc2 Two of those traders estimated the Bank of Korea to have bought around $200 million to $300 million, presumably in a bid to keep the dollar from falling beyond KRW1,057.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were last suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market a week ago, buying the dollar around KRW1,064.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is likely to maintain a moderate downward bias for next week on improving global financial market conditions, combined with expectations of fund inflows related to a KRW1.811 trillion ($1.7 billion) block sale of \uc2 KB Financial Group\uc2  treasury shares.\par \par \uc2 However, traders note the authorities seem less eager to support the dollar now compared with their recent forays in the market, probably because a strong won helps to keep imported inflation from feeding the rapid rise in domestic consumer prices.\par \par \uc2 It was "really just smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," today and the dollar's KRW1,060 support was breached easily earlier amid "a lack of any aggressive (BOK) action," said a foreign bank trader.\par \par \uc2 Immediate support for the dollar is tipped at KRW1,055 and then at KRW1,050.\par \par \uc2 Treasury bonds fell on growing optimism over the global economic recovery, with September bond futures losing 16 ticks on foreign selling.\par \par \uc2 Still, not many in the market expect bonds to post steep losses next week, with abundant liquidity in the system still providing support.\par \par \uc2 "The market's supply and demand conditions have been good for a long time now, with institutional investors--who weren't able to chalk up bonds amid falling yields--now kicking in persistently," on bargain hunting, a bond manager with an asset management firm said.\par \par \uc2 "Banks also appear to have enough money to spare" to buy bonds, he added.\par \par \uc2 -By Min-Jeong Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-3700-1908; min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com [ 08-07-11 0806GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1063 1858   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110708e778000k7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
