\par\par \b \uc2 S Korea Won Climbs To 2-Week High, Likely Triggering BOK Action\b0\par\par\uc2 451 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2011\par 10:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     Close    Change \par  USD/KRW            1,082.50 -5.80 \par  JPY/KRW            13.3527  +0.0418 \par  3-Year Treasurys   3.60%    -1 bp \par  5-Year Treasurys   3.87%    -4 bps \par  10-Year Treasurys  4.24%    -2 bps \par  20-Year Treasurys  4.43%    -1 bp \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won rose to a two-week high against the dollar Friday due to a bout of buying by offshore players amid a broad return of appetite for Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 However, the won's surge is suspected of triggering a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action during the final stages of local spot trading, which boosted the dollar from around KRW1,080 to KRW1,082.50, according to four Seoul-based traders.\par \par \uc2 After slipping to KRW1,078.90--the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lowest level since it touched KRW1,073.60 on May 11--the dollar trimmed its losses because of a combination of corporate dollar-buying and the suspected Bank of Korea move, with two traders saying they suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of buying $200 million to $400 million.\par \par \uc2 The head of the BOK's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market team declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The suspected BOK action shows that despite the lingering euro-zone debt concerns and domestic inflationary pressures, the authorities' "basic mind-set" that undue won strength will hurt the nation's export-driven economy hasn't changed, said a trader at a foreign bank.\par \par \uc2 It appears the authorities can't take a KRW20 fall in the dollar in just two days, he added. The dollar had slipped to KRW1,088.30 Thursday from KRW1,101.80 Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Traders said they expect the dollar to trade in a KRW1,070-KRW1,090 range next week.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were last suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on May 2, likely buying the dollar near the KRW1,069 and KRW1,066 levels, traders say.\par \par \uc2 Treasury bonds and bond futures rose Friday, lifted by active foreign buying in futures. June bond futures gained 10 ticks to 103.70 as foreigners snapped up 6,766 contracts in net.\par \par \uc2 Foreigners "could be betting on a BOK rate freeze" in June given the on-going euro-zone debt troubles, while on the domestic front, household debt remains high, said a bond manager with a local securities firm.\par \par \uc2 For now, foreigners appear to be trying to trigger a bout of short covering from other investors to take profit, he added.\par \par \uc2 He set near-term resistance for futures at 103.80.\par \par \uc2 -By Min-Jeong Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-3700-1908; min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com [ 27-05-11 0824GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1063 1858   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110527e75r000k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
