\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Of Korea May Have Bought $300M-$500M To Curb Won's Gains - Traders\b0\par\par\uc2 178 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2011\par 08:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Korea may have bought around $300 million to $500 million curb the won's gains earlier Monday, according to two Seoul-based traders.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to have bought the dollar mostly near the KRW1,069 and KRW1,066 levels in early Asia trade, three Seoul based traders said.\par \par \uc2 The head of the BOK's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market team declined to comment.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were last suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on April 28, likely buying the dollar near KRW1,071 and KRW1,072 levels throughout the session, traders say.\par \par \uc2 Despite the suspected BOK \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar was down at KRW1,065.00 late Monday in Asia, compared with KRW1,071.50 late Friday, weighed by export settlements and following news of a larger than expected trade surplus for April.\par \par \uc2 - By Min-Jeong Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-3700-1908; min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com [ 02-05-11 0654GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110502e752000dn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
