\par\par \b \uc2 S Korea Won Up At 32-Month High; BOK Likely  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  Again; Bonds Up\b0\par\par\uc2 534 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2011\par 09:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     Close    Change \par  USD/KRW            1,071.20 -8.30 \par  JPY/KRW            13.1082  -0.1080 \par  3-Year Treasurys   3.79%    -3 bps \par  5-Year Treasurys   4.14%    -3 bps \par  10-Year Treasurys  4.52%    -2 bps \par  20-Year Treasurys  4.67%    -2 bps \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won surged to a fresh 32-month high against the dollar Thursday after the U.S. Federal Reserve signalled it isn't looking to shift its loose stance anytime soon, sparking a suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  action for the second straight day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea is suspected of having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the dollar near the KRW1,071 and KRW1,072 levels throughout the session, according to eight Seoul-based traders.\par \par \uc2 Three of the traders estimate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought more than $500 million worth of dollars, mostly near the KRW1,071 level.\par \par \uc2 The suspected BOK action was presumably an attempt to keep the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above KRW1,070, as authorities appear concerned that a breach of that support may lead the dollar to swiftly fall to as low as the KRW1,050 level, traders say.\par \par \uc2 The head of the BOK's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market team declined to comment.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were last suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market just a day ago on Wednesday, likely buying the dollar near KRW1,079.\par \par \uc2 Still, a local bank trader advised investors to "stay put" with any short-positions on the dollar as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to continue its "stable downward trend" ahead.\par \par \uc2 "There's an awful lot of dollar-offers at higher levels like KRW1,073, with a string of sales orders from exporters waiting," on top of a number of dollar bears, which will likely continue to pressure the dollar down, said another local bank trader.\par \par \uc2 The government also tried to talk down the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though such efforts were in vain, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Deputy Minister of Strategy and Finance for Foreign Affairs Choi Jong-ku said Thursday the government has seen that offshore \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants are betting on the Korean won to rise further, accelerating the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation, and may adopt stricter rules to stem it.\par \par \uc2 Despite the deputy finance minister's remarks and suspected BOK action, the dollar slipped at one point to an intraday low of KRW1,071.00, marking its lowest since it hit KRW1,064.10 on August 25, 2008.\par \par \uc2 Treasury bonds and bond futures rose as foreigners snapped a six-session selling streak in the futures market, in wake of the won's surge to a 32-month high.\par \par \uc2 June bond futures gained 13 ticks to 102.94 as foreigners bought a net 3,759 contracts.\par \par \uc2 However, some traders doubt the sustainability of any bond price gains as they think the Fed's ultra-loose monetary policy, which would persist for some time, may do more harm than good for safe-haven assets as global stock markets may extend their rally.\par \par \uc2 - By Min-Jeong Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 