\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-S.Korean's inflation fears grow, banks brace for won crackdown\b0\par\par\uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2011\par 11:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Inflation expectations hit 4 percent, top of BOK range\par \par \uc2  * Korea to cut banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives limits after foreign debt jump\par \par \uc2  * Won sees biggest loss in two weeks on plans to curb derivatives \par \par \uc2 (Recasts with central bankers, capital controls)\par \par \uc2  By Yoo Choonsik\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, April 26 - South Koreans expect inflation to remain at the top end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target over the next year, indicating it has  failed to dampen concerns over rising prices, as authorities looked set to take action to curb the rising won \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a surge in foreign debt.\par \par \uc2  Consumers' inflation expectations -- the median inflation forecast for the next year -- rose to 4.0 percent in April, a 22-month high, from 3.8 percent in a March survey, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2  The top end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation target is 4 percent.\par \par \uc2  "These indicators ... back the consensus view that more interest rates may be needed to contain inflation expectations," said Kim Jin-seong, an economist at Hanwha Securities.  \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged at its April meeting despite clear signs of unease among its members over mounting inflation, which raced to a 29-month high of 4.7 percent in March.\par \par \uc2  Minutes of its March meeting, also released on Tuesday, showed policymakers believed that delaying a rate hike "would be a very dangerous policy decision".\par \par \uc2  At that meeting, the policy board voted 4-1 to raise rates\par \par \uc2 by a quarter point to 3.0 percent. One policymaker who was not identified in the documents warned the annual inflation rate "could soon rise above 5 percent."\par \par \uc2  While some traders maintain the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been sending confusing signals about its intentions, other market watchers believe Seoul recently opted for a more direct tactic to contain price pressures, grudgingly allowing the won to rise to curb imported inflation.\par \par \uc2  But authorities now appear to be readying to apply the brakes on the won, fearful that speculation on further won gains is creating a potentially destabilising surge in short-term foreign debt.\par \par \uc2  Banks have been borrowing to fund forward \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  deals with local exporters.\par \par \uc2  Two sources with direct knowledge of the plans told Reuters that South Korea will likely trim the ceilings on banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives positions by at least one-fifth after an inspection of banks is concluded next week. [ID:nL3E7FQ0UO]\par \par \uc2  "Once the government were to change them, the ceilings will likely be (reduced to) 200 percent and 40 percent as widely expected in the markets," said one source, compared with 250 percent of equity set for foreign bank branches and 50 percent for domestic banks.\par \par \uc2  The won suffered its worst daily loss on Tuesday in two weeks after the moves were mooted.\par \par \uc2  The won has jumped around 5 percent against the dollar in less than a month to hit a 31-month high last week but has since then retreated slightly, partly on talk of massive dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at stopping the surge.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  is inspecting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivative trades by four banks -- including two foreign bank branches -- and looks close to trimming by at least one-fifth the maximum amount of derivatives each bank can carry.\par \par \uc2  CONSUMER SENTIMENT IMPROVES\par \par \uc2  Despite the growing worries that high inflation will persist well into next year, other data on Tuesday showed consumers may be more resilient than first thought.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's consumer sentiment index, which measures how South Koreans assess the economic outlook and their future living standards, rose to 100 in April after hitting 98 in March, when the reading was at its lowest since April 2009.\par \par \uc2  The index reversed a four-month falling streak since hitting 110 in November last year. A reading above 100 means those consumers who were optimistic about the future economy and living standards outnumber the pessimists.\par \par \uc2  Analysts said the survey results underscored the market's view that the Bank of Korea would deliver a fifth interest rate increase as early as next month, despite its reluctance to back a rise in April.\par \par \uc2  South Korea has Also frozen public utility charges, pressured refiners to cut fuel prices and removed tariffs on some meat imports to contain inflation. \par \par \uc2  The consensus view among economists is for another rate raise in May or June and one more later in the year, taking the benchmark rate to 3.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Lee Shin-hyung; Editing by David Chance and Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 KOREA-ECONOMY/SENTIMENT (WRAPUP 1, PIX)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110426e74q000kg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
