\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Asia Central Banks  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  Again As Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Climb\b0\par\par\uc2 By Martin Vaughan  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2011\par 09:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Central banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Thailand and Malaysia \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday to slow a surge in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a broad Asian rally that carried the Singapore and Australian dollars to fresh highs against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2 's decision to fix the yuan at a fresh high record against the U.S. dollar added to bullish views on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which have been trending upward as central banks around the region tighten monetary policies to fight rising inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally Wednesday appeared to be driven by a rebound in risk-taking sentiment. Recovering from temporary misgivings about U.S. fiscal health following \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  rating warning, investors warmed to risk again, pumping more money into Asia's fast-growing economies.\par \par \uc2 Wai Ho Leong, senior regional economist at Barclay's Capital, said improved risk appetite and also continued high oil prices are some of the factors influencing Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' move higher. High commodity prices "give policymakers the willingness to tolerate a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 " as a check on inflation, he said.\par \par \uc2 Many Asian countries have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months to stem a decline in the U.S. dollar, which could hurt their export industries. The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Korean, Thai and Malaysian authorities Wednesday appeared to be aimed at tempering gains in the won, baht and ringgit rather than an effort to stop the upward trend in those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar reached a new post-float high against the U.S. dollar, fetching US$1.0611 before easing back to $1.0602. Its climb was in part driven by a Dow Jones Newswires report that Australia is weighing a tax break on passive investments by sovereign funds.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar also notched record highs, as the greenback fell to S$1.2400. The Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nearly year-long rally gained momentum last week when the island's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  revalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pledged to continue to guide it higher to curb rising inflation.\par \par \uc2 The PBOC, which tightly controls the yuan, fixed the dollar's exchange rate at CNY6.5294, down from Tuesday's fixing of CNY6.5346 and close of CNY6.5305 in the over-the-counter market. Traders said they expect the PBOC to let the yuan rise further ahead of a May 24-25 U.S.-China strategic economic dialogue in Beijing, since China has a record of yuan strengthening ahead of such international events.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea likely bought about $500 million Wednesday in an effort to curb the won's ascent, traders said. The dollar dipped to KRW1,082.00 before ticking up to KRW1,082.20 late in Asian trade.\par \par \uc2 Against the Malaysian ringgit, the U.S. dollar fell to MYR3.0160, its lowest level since September 1997. That was down from MYR3.0230 at Tuesday's close. The ringgit rebounded somewhat on buying by \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2 , traders in Kuala Lumpur said. The dollar was last at MYR3.0155, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of more buying at that level.\par \par \uc2 Further gains could be in store for the ringgit, as it has lagged recently against regional peers like the Singapore dollar, which it tends to track closely. Continued high oil prices will support the ringgit, as will expectations of a rate hike at Bank Negara's next policy meeting, set for May 5.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  was suspected of buying dollars as the baht gained ahead of a monetary policy meeting at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates by 25 basis points--as expected--and said interest rates remain on an upward trend. The action helped keep the dollar supported at THB30.02, traders said.\par \par \uc2 -By Martin Vaughan, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 6415 4033; martin.vaughan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -K.P. Lee and Min-Jeong Lee contributed to this article\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 20, 2011 03:51 ET (07:51 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110420e74k001iv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
