\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 South Korea \highlight\uc2  won at 2-1/2 yr high, checked by  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 557 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2011\par 10:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  more frequent than usual - dealers\par \par \uc2 * Treasury futures pare most of daily loss as KOSPI retreats\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, April 6 (Reuters) - The South Korean won rose to a 2-1/2 year high against the dollar on Wednesday, lifted by steady fund flows into local stocks and bonds, with an expected European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate increase dragging on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities were spotted during the day, checking the pace of the won's advance as foreign investors remained net buyers of Seoul shares for a 16th session, the longest buying streak since last October.\par \par \uc2 "Today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was more frequent than other days, and included last-minute dollar buying," said a foreign bank dealer. "But foreign investors continued to buy local stocks, and (dollar) short sentiment was strong on dollar/won and other cross rates."\par \par \uc2 South Korea's top financial watchdog cautioned against the recent fund inflows, saying that more than half of foreign purchases of domestic stocks were short-term investments following last month's massive earthquake in Japan. [ID:nL3E7F60OU]\par \par \uc2 The won settled at 1,086.8 per dollar, after strengthening to as much as 1,083.8, the highest since Sept. 10, 2008.\par \par \uc2 On the year, it has risen by 4.4 percent, one of the two best-performing emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has been in oversold territory against the won, with the 14-day Relative Strength Index below the oversold mark of 30 since March 30.\par \par \uc2 Dollar/won has also been below the 200-day moving average, indicating further weakness in the dollar as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks all but certain to lift rates for the first time since July 2008 on Thursday to curb inflation.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors bought a net 996 billion won of domestic bonds in March, up slightly from February's level, according to the regulatory Financial Supervisory Service. But they cashed in a net 772 billion won of shares last month, down sharply from selling 3.7 trillion won in February.\par \par \uc2 Bond curves steepened slightly, with front-end yields steady on the consensus view that the Bank of Korea will likely leave the base rate unchanged next week.\par \par \uc2 Treasury futures prices narrowed losses in afternoon trade as the Seoul stock market pulled back from a record high set on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "The government reaffirmed that price pressures would likely soften from April," said a local brokerage dealer. "A rate rise looks difficult to happen this month."\par \par \uc2 In another administrative effort to tame inflation pressures, the South Korean government said it would consider slashing oil taxes if crude prices rose further. [ID:nL3E7F60NV] \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                            Close    Prev close \tab \par Dollar/won                 1,086.8       1,090.2 \tab \par Yen/won                12.7163/296   12.9188/263 \tab \par *KTB futures                103.09        103.12 \tab \par 5-yr treasury bonds       4.07 pct      4.05 pct         \tab \par 3-yr treasury bonds       3.70 pct      3.69 pct      \tab \par Average O/N call rate     2.98 pct      3.10 pct  \tab \par ^6-mth KORIBOR            3.54 pct      3.54 pct  \tab \par KOSPI                     2,126.71      2,130.43 \tab \par \f28 \par \par