\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 South Korea \highlight\uc2  loosens grip on won as inflation spikes\b0\par\par\uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2011\par 23:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  infrequently despite won's fast rise\par \par \uc2 * Inflation a hot issue ahead of elections\par \par \uc2 * Loosened grip seen as temporary - analysts\par \par \uc2 (Recasts with analysts' comments, details)\par \par \uc2 By Yoo Choonsik\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, April 4 (Reuters) - A surprisingly small increase in foreign reserves in March has highlighted South Korea's decision to loosen its grip on the rising won to curb inflation, though Seoul's tolerance of a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not last long.\par \par \uc2 Analysts and traders believe the shift in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was largely a short-term one, aimed at calming public anger over quickening inflation ahead of key by-elections later this month.\par \par \uc2 The won hit a low for the year on March 17 but has since has risen 4.4 percent on the U.S. dollar to around 1,086. The sharp run-up has surprised hedge funds and other investors who had expected authorities to continue to rein in the Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ensure robust export growth.\par \par \uc2 "The pace of appreciation in the past couple of weeks has certainly been impressive, but it will eventually come to a pause and the won will consolidate back to a more sustainable pace of appreciation," Sacha Tihanyi, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's inflation in February was double the average rate for members of the Organisation for Economic Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Development (OECD). In March it picked up further to a 29-month high of 4.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 The government has pledged to keep inflation at around 3 percent on average for the whole of the year, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has forecast inflation would average at 3.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Growing price pressures have emerged as a hot issue as President Lee Myung-bak's party faces tough competition from the opposition in by-elections later this month in several regions, which will be a test for next year's presidential race.\par \par \uc2 Bitter memories of a near sovereign insolvency in the late 1990s, sparked in part by an overvalued won and big current account deficits, have since led South Korea to maintain zero tolerance to any sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.\par \par \uc2 MEMORIES OF 1997-98 CRISIS\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data released on Monday showed some signs of a change in policy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in Asia's fourth-largest economy rose only $0.95 billion last month even as the won rose the most in six months.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea attributed the gain to strength in non-dollar assets and investment gains. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials do not comment on market talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The reserves were at a record $298.62 billion in March, some 30 percent of the country's annual gross domestic product that passed $1 trillion last year.\par \par \uc2 The country's foreign reserves added $1.71 billion in February and $4.39 billion in January after reports by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders of government dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at smoothing the won's rise.\par \par \uc2 The small rise in reserves also came as the won rose nearly 3 percent against the dollar in March, making it the best month since September last year.\par \par \uc2 Traders on Monday reported South Korean authorities were buying dollars as the won rose to its strongest level against the dollar since early September 2008, but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not so strong as to wipe out all of the won's gains.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said South Korean authorities, mindful of a firmer won's impact on the country's exporters, could turn more active in checking the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp appreciation should oil and other raw materials prices show signs of easing.\par \par \uc2 "The authorities will see the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ease anyway once oil prices rose further above $120 (per barrel) and the yen kept weakening (against the dollar)," a veteran \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader in Seoul said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders would comment mostly on condition they are not named.\par \par \uc2 Even if upward pressure on the won lasted a long time, the government of President Lee Myung-bak would probably tighten its grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against after the April 27 by-elections, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's foreign reserves ranked seventh in the world at the end of February.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Kim Yeonhee and Lee Soo-jung in SEOUL, Jongwoo Cheon in SINGAPORE; Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 KOREA-ECONOMY/RESERVES (UPDATE 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110403e743000r6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
