\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won leads other Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  higher as Q1 ends\b0\par\par\uc2 1,034 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 04:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Won breaks through 1,100 on exporters, stop-loss dlr sales\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from S.Korea, Taiwan, Malaysia, Singapore spotted\par \par \uc2 * Some dealers cautious about near-term correction\par \par \uc2 (Updates with text, prices)\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, March 31 (Reuters) - The South Korean won rose to a 2-1/2-year high on Thursday, leading other emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, on the back of sustained inflows into the Seoul stock market which will probably spell further gains for the won in the coming quarter.\par \par \uc2 The won was the biggest gainer among regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first three months of the year, advancing 3.6 percent against the dollar, followed by the Indonesian rupiah and the Taiwan dollar.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's tumble below 1,100 won late in the session spilled over to other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Thai baht and Singapore dollar, causing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Taiwan and Malaysia to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' gains, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected to strengthen further in the second quarter, with foreign equity flows to the region rebounding after a selloff earlier in March following a massive earthquake and tsunami in Japan.\par \par \uc2 "Fears about monetary tightening and inflation risks in the region appear to be easing, with the growth outlook in the region remaining robust," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Credit Agricole in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "We look for a soft landing for China's economy, which should also help the positive momentum for capital flows and in turn Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 In the near term, though, the recent rallies in some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may result in corrective moves lower, some analysts and dealers said.\par \par \uc2 The won's gains have been bolstered by a 12-day long Korean equity buying spree by foreign investors -- the longest streak since October 2010.\par \par \uc2 "A correction (in emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is possible. I think the recent gains are very over stretched. The whole world is too comfortable with short USD/Asia," a Kuala Lumpur-based dealer said.\par \par \uc2 In local trade, the dollar/won's 14-day relative strength index fell to 31.548, the lowest since Oct. 2010, indicating the dollar is approaching oversold territory against the won.\par \par \uc2 The RSI for the rupiah, the second-best performer in the first quarter, has been under 30 since early February.\par \par \uc2 RUPIAH\par \par \uc2 One-year dollar/rupiah non-deliverable forwards (NDF)\par \par \uc2 slid to 9,080, its weakest since June 2007 as speculative investors bet on the rupiah's further appreciation.\par \par \uc2 "Indonesia's growth will attract more FDI inflows and also possible upgrades of its sovereign rating," said a Jakarta-based dealer, citing reasons for the rupiah's climb.\par \par \uc2 In the spot market, the rupiah , which had earlier suffered from dollar-short covering, tested 8,700, beyond which would be the strongest since May 2007.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah may appreciate further in the medium term as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to allow more gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight inflation, and on foreign investors' appetite for the country's bonds.\par \par \uc2 Foreign ownership of Indonesian government bonds stood at 31.25 percent as of Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 TAIWAN DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar rose as exporters bought it for settlements, but its gains were capped as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted buying dollars, especially around T$29.45 and T$29.55, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Market players also stayed cautious ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate-setting meeting later in the day where it is widely expected to raise interest rates by 12.5 basis points to 1.75 percent.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to maintain its momentum into the second quarter thanks to hopes of growing foreign fund inflows, dealers in Taipei said.\par \par \uc2 PESO\par \par \uc2 The peso started local trade at 43.35 per dollar but cut some gains as local banks sold the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of domestic companies.\par \par \uc2 The peso's support line of 43.50 was no challenged amidst current risk on sentiment. A bullish euro and Australian dollar also provide support the peso.\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar hit a fresh record high of 1.2590 per dollar on broad weakness in the U.S. dollar and as the firmer won caused investors to keep chasing the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted buying the greenback to check the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR                                  \tab \par  Change on the day at 0740 GMT    \tab \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        82.80          82.88       +0.10\par  Sing dlr        1.2596         1.2622       +0.21\par  Taiwan dlr      29.397         29.510       +0.38\par  Korean won     1095.80        1104.20       +0.77\par  Baht             30.23          30.32       +0.30\par  Peso             43.41          43.44       +0.08\par  Rupiah         8703.00        8716.00       +0.15\par  Rupee            44.64          44.75       +0.24\par  Ringgit         3.0250         3.0255       +0.02\par  Yuan            6.5488         6.5559       +0.11\par                                                                                                \par  Change so far in 2011                                                                         \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        82.80          81.15       -1.99\par  Sing dlr        1.2596         1.2820       +1.78\par  Taiwan dlr      29.397         30.368       +3.30\par  Korean won     1095.80        1134.80       +3.56\par  Baht             30.23          30.14       -0.30\par  Peso             43.41          43.84       +1.00\par  Rupiah         8703.00        9005.00       +3.47\par  Rupee            44.64          44.70       +0.13\par  Ringgit         3.0250         3.0820       +1.88\par  Yuan            6.5488         6.5897       +0.62\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Lee Yingchien in TAIPEI, Lee Soo-jung in SEOUL and Catherine Tan of IFR Markets; Editing by Kim Coghill)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the new Reuters scrolling global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  service please click\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Double click on brackets for following items: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b