\par\par \b \uc2 S Korea Won Up Sharply As G-7 Vows Joint Action; Most Bonds Down\b0\par\par\uc2 486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 08:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     Close    Change \par  USD/KRW           1,126.60  -8.70 \par  JPY/KRW           13.7861   -0.5431 \par  3-Year Treasurys   3.65%    -1 bp \par  5-Year Treasurys   4.07%    +2 bps \par  10-Year Treasurys  4.46%    +5 bps \par  20-Year Treasurys  4.59%    +5 bps \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won rose sharply against the dollar and the yen Friday as the Group of Seven industrial powers vowed joint action in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to ensure stability, a move that could encourage the Bank of Korea to similarly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up the won.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Though \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't in the grouping, "such an agreement amongst G-7 countries is leading the market to think that South Korean authorities are now likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more freely" to support the won after its steep fall, a local bank trader said. The won had fallen on risk aversion sparked by last week's massive quake and ensuing nuclear concerns in Japan.\par \par \uc2 The Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of stepping into the market Tuesday and Thursday in support of the won in a bid to reduce market volatility arising from the disaster in Japan.\par \par \uc2 Seoul-based traders, however, say that the recent efforts by the local authorities to stem the won's declines don't necessarily mean the government is actually gunning for a stronger local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It is of course a boxed trading range that the authorities want," which appears to be between KRW1,100 and KRW1,140 to a dollar, a foreign bank trader said, tipping the range to be where the greenback will trade next week.\par \par \uc2 Such a range suggests the authorities could step in to buy the dollar if it falls below KRW1,110 and sell the greenback if it rises above KRW1,130, he said.\par \par \uc2 As of 0750 GMT, the yen was sharply lower at KRW13.7748 from KRW14.3292 late Thursday, as a bout of stop-loss sell orders were triggered in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  following the yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japan in early trade, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "How aggressive (any) joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by G-7 will be" may determine the yen's near-term movement against the won, said Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji, who tips near-term support at KRW13.4000.\par \par \uc2 Most Treasury bonds fell, with the yield curve turning steeper after its recent flattening.\par \par \uc2 "Longer-end bonds had strengthened too much recently, with concerns over the global economic recovery," boosting the appetite for those bonds, said a bond manager with a securities firm.\par \par \uc2 He expects the yield curve to steepen slightly further until early next week, ahead of the government's auction of 10- and 20-year bonds coming up later this month.\par \par \uc2 - By Min-Jeong Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-3700-1908; min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com [ 18-03-11 0759GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1063 1858   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110318e73i000l8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
