\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Hit Multi-Year Highs As Risk Appetite Revives\b0\par\par\uc2 963 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2011\par 09:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  at multi-year highs on revived risk appetite\par \par \uc2 -- Expectations grow that central banks will fight inflation with stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -- Korea, Malaysia suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' rises\par \par \uc2 (Adds Korea and Malaysia \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , updates market rates.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Arran Scott and Mark Cranfield \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones) Several Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hit multi-year highs against the dollar Tuesday as revived risk appetite encouraged investors to plow money back into the region.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The authorities in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Malaysia were spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, local traders said, marking the latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Asian authorities to slow the rise in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Flows are coming back into Asia mainly because investors are betting on the strong (economic) fundamentals in the region," said a trader at a foreign bank in Singapore.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Higher Asia-Pacific share prices, triggered in part by strength on Wall Street overnight and a decline in crude oil prices, set a bullish tone for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Investors appear to be buying into the notion Asian central banks will use a combination of stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and higher interest rates this year to temper inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Malaysian ringgit and Taiwan dollar rose to their highest levels since October 1997, when the Asian financial crisis sent regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  into a tailspin. The Korean won, which has lagged in the broad Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation last year, struck levels not seen since the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lehman Brothers\f28 \uc2 \f2  collapse in September 2008 clobbered Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indonesia rupiah added to large gains it has made since Friday, when the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raised interest rates and signaled it would use a stronger local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to rein in inflation that has surged above its target band. The rupiah had been hurt by concerns that Jakarta was falling behind the curve in containing inflation, but has now nearly recovered the losses it had logged since mid-November when such worries began to escalate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the exception of the Thai baht and Indian rupee, most major regional Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have posted gains so far this year, with the ringgit, rupiah, won and Taiwan dollar leading a modest appreciation on par with gains marked early last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation has been picking up in Asia, fueled by fast economic growth, loose monetary policies and soaring prices of food and commodities on global markets. Central banks across the region have raised interest rates and tried to soak up liquidity that has gushed into the region as investors hunt for higher returns than those available in low interest-rate environments in the U.S. and Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The final tool in the armory of the region's authorities is \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation," Fiona Lake, an analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2 , wrote in a research note. "Korea, Indonesia and Taiwan have shown greater desire for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation to help tackle inflation."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The won has some catching up to do with Asia's strongest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the past year: the Singapore dollar, the ringgit and the baht. The Bank of Korea has also turned more hawkish in its comments on tackling rising inflation. Economists in a Dow Jones Newswires poll were split on whether the BOK will raise rates again Friday after a surprise rate hike last month. Among those predicting a tightening, Nomura tips a quarter percentage-point increase this week, with similar moves in April and June.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What's more, the U.S. has put the won on its list of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for which "there is room for a greater degree of exchange-rate stability and less \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ." The Treasury Department, in its annual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  report Friday, cited International Monetary Fund calculations that the won is 5%-20% undervalued.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Washington, seeking stronger Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to put U.S. exporters on a better competitive footing, likely has more leverage with Korea than with China. Sean Callow, a strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2 , points out that Congress hasn't ratified the U.S.-South Korea free trade agreement signed by Presidents Barack Obama and Lee Myung-bak in December.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was at KRW1,104.70 Tuesday afternoon in Asia, down from KRW1,107.50 late Monday, tracking the regional decline. But its fall was limited as traders said the BOK bought an estimated $300-$500 million to brake the won's ascent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Similarly, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  bought an estimated $200 million, traders said, after the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  carved out a fresh 13-year low against the ringgit, last trading at MYR3.0330. That was down from MYR3.0360 late Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against the Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was last at NT$28.9080, after earlier touching NT$28.900, its lowest since October 1997, on foreign capital flows into Taipei stocks on the first trading day after the Lunar New Year holidays. A trader said the market is on the lookout for the Taiwan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which is thought to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regularly near the close of trading to smooth out moves in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against the rupiah, the dollar was around IDR8,915, down from IDR8,960 Monday and back near the IDR8,890 level at which it traded in mid-November, before concerns over inflation started to permeate the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against the Singapore dollar, which has recently logged record highs, the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was at SGD1.2728, down from SGD1.2746 late Monday\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The baht and Philippine peso were also higher, helped by the broader rally in Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Arran Scott and Mark Cranfield, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4042; arran.scott@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Min-Jeong Lee in Seoul, Ankur Relia in Kuala Lumpur, I Made Sentana in Jakarta, Fanny Liu in Taipei and P.R. Venkat in Singapore contributed to this article. [ 08-02-11 0801GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110208e728000pg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
