\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/MYR Lower Late; 3.0320-3.0380 Tipped Tuesday\b0\par\par\uc2 3,783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 2011\par 08:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0746 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR falls to a fresh 13-year low at 3.0360 vs 3.0420 late Wednesday, tracking the USD's weakness vs the EUR. Two dealers say the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is suspected to have been buying dollars at various levels, and most recently at 3.0360, effectively keeping the support level at 3.0360 firm. "Selling hasn't been so aggressive in the afternoon with the presence of Bank Negara," says one dealer, adding that as sentiment continues to favor a stronger MYR, the pair may be headed lower in the near-term. He tips a the pair to trade in a 3.0320-3.0380 range Tuesday. Maybank IB Research says the pair is likely to trend lower based on charts after it breached the key 3.0775 level, with the next support levels at 3.0200, then 3.0000; and 2.9750 in the longer-term. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Corrected February 7, 2011 03:11 ET (08:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 0723 GMT [Dow Jones] So long as China keeps allowing some CNY appreciation, Treasury will continue to face difficulty slapping the label of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator on Beijing without new legislation to facilitate doing so, says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Sean Callow; the U.S. Treasury's semi-annual \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  report released Friday declared the CNY "substantially undervalued," but as expected didn't name China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator. "Tougher action on China is only plausible (and still unlikely) under new legislation, momentum on which has slowed since the November mid-term elections." That, Callow says, is despite the fact that Treasury has added an argument about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on USD/CNY equating to Beijing's borrowing from its public. Meanwhile, the Treasury report takes a tougher line on \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This could matter for the KRW, Callow says, as any further BOK \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "would certainly not win any new votes" for the U.S.-South Korea free trade agreement that needs to be ratified by Congress to take effect. (toko.sekiguchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0657 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI up 0.3% at 1535.83 in early afternoon trade. A local dealer says buying interest remains strong with investors returning to the market after the Lunar New Year break. Plantation stocks are leading the way on a stronger CPO outlook. "There's quite a bit of institutional buying but regional sentiment is turning a tad negative, so we would be a little cautious... there could be some profit-taking later today," the dealer says, adding that foreign funds are also retruning to the market after the sell-off in late January. The USD/MYR hit a fresh 13-year low at 3.0360 earlier today from 3.0420 late Wednesday. The dealer tips resistance at 1550, with support at 1530. The market breadth is positive with 411 advancers compared with 337 decliners. Among advancers \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) is up 5.2% at MYR22.72, Axiata (6888.KU) is up 1.9% at MYR4.95, and Maybank (1155.KU) is up 0.8% at MYR8.80. Among decliners Genting (3182.KU) is down 3.2% at MYR10.98, and \uc2 Malaysia Marine & Heavy Engineering\uc2  (5186.KU) is off 0.8% at MYR6.01. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is down 0.5% at 23,794.14, succumbing to profit-taking as the index's 1.8% spike in half-day of trade Wednesday before the long holiday appears overdone. Market volume is light at HK$40.20 billion and it's still lower than Wednesday's half-day volume, suggesting that profit-taking pressure is not heavy; the 50-day moving average at 23,418 appears quite safe. Everbright Research pegs the HSI's 2011 fair value at 25,800 which represents 14X forward P/E, but it adds that if market sentiment turns more optimistic on potential fund inflow, then the HSI may trade up to 16X P/E, translating into 29,470-point index target. Most blue chips are lower but Esprit (0330.HK) extends gains in afternoon and the stock rises 4.1% to HK$39.30, as investors bet on solid 1H results due Thursday. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi ends +0.5% at 2081.74, but it's well off an intraday high of 2017.83 as recent outperformers including shipbuilders and energy stocks fall on profit-taking. "Investors remain wary amid growing concerns over inflation in emerging markets and possible rate hikes in China and Korea this month," says Bae Sung-young at \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2 . He adds, if the USD/KRW falls below 1,100, it could hurt export-oriented plays such as technology stocks and auto shares. The USD/KRW fell to 1107.50 vs 1116.90 on Feb 1. "But long-term investors won't leave emerging economies due to their growth potential and revival in consumption," he says. The benchmark index is tipped to trade in a 2050-2180 band this week. Among losers, \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) is down 1.1% at KRW972,000, \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2  (009540.SE) is down 2.5% at KRW474,000 and \uc2 SK Innovation\uc2  (096770.SE) is off 3.2% at KRW195,000. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0634 GMT [Dow Jones] The Swiss January CPI is likely to be unchanged to slightly lower, with increases in administrative and oil prices offset by lower clothing prices due to the winter sales, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "Of interest for monetary policy will be the domestic components and particularly the sub-series on services prices, making up 58% of the goods basket of Swiss consumers," says economist Reto Huenerwadel. "In light of the above, we expect the inflation differential between the EU area and Switzerland to reach 200 basis points, i.e. a level reached only on two prior occasions in the last 20-odd years," he says. The CPI data are due for release on Feb. 10 at 0815 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0627 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD extends its decline to 7.7826 from 7.7845 earlier Monday, below 7.7884 late Wednesday, as funds flow into Asian markets after the Lunar New Year holiday, says a senior trader at a local bank. "Investors' eyes are on rosy Chinese equities and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the markets are set to open this Wednesday. The positive sentiment supports the Hong Kong dollar," though a drop in the city's stock market is limiting the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gain. He tips the pair to trade between 7.7800-7.7850 in the session. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0625 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Japan's current account surplus may rise to Y1.159 trillion in December, up 26.6% from Y915.6 billion in the year-earlier period, according to a Dow Jones and Nikkei poll of economists. In November, the figure fell 15.7% on-year. Mizuho Research Institute's economist Yusuke Ichikawa says "we expect a greater trade surplus as well as returns from Japan's direct investments overseas." December data will be released along with FY10 data at 2350 GMT Monday. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0625 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly higher; Nikkei +0.5%; S&P/ASX +0.1%; Kospi +0.5%; Sensex +0.3%; HSI down 0.5%; STI down 0.3%; Indonesian shares +0.1%; Philippine shares +0.2%; SET down 0.6%; NZX-50 +0.6%; KLCI +0.2%. China and Taiwan markets are closed for a holiday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the EUR rose against the USD, and JPY as generally stronger regional equity markets encouraged buying of the risk-sensitive common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The EUR/USD is at 1.3605 from 1.3582 in NY late Friday, the EUR/JPY is at 111.90 from 111.67, and the USD/JPY is at 82.23 from 82.20. BOJ Governor Shirakawa says ample liquidity alone will not solve deflation, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue its current programs to help revive the economy. Japan December leading indicator +0.8 points on-month 101.4. Japan December leading indicators +0.8 points on-month. Australia January ANZ total job ads +2.4% on-month vs +1.2% in December. Australia December adjusted retail sales +0.2% on-month vs +0.4% in November, +0.5% expected. Indonesia 4Q GDP +6.9% on-year vs +5.8% in 3Q, +6.2% expected. Hong Kong January \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI at 55.2 vs 55.0 in December. Spot gold is at $1,346.60/oz, down $2.00 from the NY close. March Nymex crude oil futures are up 22 cents at $89.25/bbl.(john.phillips@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0622 GMT [Dow Jones] India's FY11 GDP growth estimate of 8.6% following two consecutive quarters of 8.9% growth indicates a slowdown in industrial and investment activity, says Anubhuti Sahay, an economist at Standard Chartered Bank. She says the moderation in growth is likely to continue for a few months into FY12. "Considering the moderation going forward, we expect the RBI to stick to its calibrated tightening of monetary policy; i.e. a 25 basis point increase in both rates at the March 17 and May 2 meetings," she says. She adds, a rise in global crude oil prices poses an upside risk to inflation, which could warrant an aggressive move depending on the inflationary trend. India's previous GDP growth estimate for FY11 was 8.5%. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT (Dow Jones) USD/CHF's recovery may stall temporarily at the 0.9599 level, though upside potential to 0.9646 remains, according to \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  technical analysts. "Last week saw a strong rebound once again from the low 0.9300 area, reinforcing the support there and adding weight to the idea the market has based ahead of the 0.9318 zone," says analyst Karen Jones. "Longer term we regard the market as attempting to base and favour recovery to 0.9784, then 1.0067," she adds. USD/CHF is at 0.9538. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com) [ 02-07-11 0246ET ]\par \par \uc2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] India's fiscal-year GDP growth estimate of 8.6% is broadly in-line with expectations, although the expected deceleration in industry and manufacturing output does throw up a surprise, says Abheek Barua, chief economist at \uc2 HDFC Bank\uc2 . "Although the estimate and the pattern of growth in different sectors is on expected lines, the risk is that if manufacturing and industry continue to decelerate, then we will have a much lower headline growth number for the next fiscal year," Barua says. GDP growth could slow to 8.0% in fiscal FY12 from this year's government projection of 8.6% if manufacturing decelerates, he adds, while noting farm and services sectors are showing strong growth. "Given a combination of a low base and good rainfall this year, kharif (summer crop) was reasonably strong and rabi (winter crop) expectations are also fairly robust," he says. India expects 8.8% growth in manufacturing this fiscal year and a sharp rebound in farm output growth to 5.4% from 0.4% last fiscal year. (anant.kala@dowjones.com) [ 02-07-11 0246ET ]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0803 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0746 GMT incorrectly stated the level at which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was suspected of buying dollars, as well as the USD/MYR's support level. The correct version follows:) The USD/MYR falls to a fresh 13-year low at 3.0360 vs 3.0420 late Wednesday, tracking the USD's weakness vs the EUR. Two dealers say the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is suspected to have been buying dollars at various levels, and most recently at 3.0360, effectively keeping the support level at 3.0360 firm. "Selling hasn't been so aggressive in the afternoon with the presence of Bank Negara," says one dealer, adding that as sentiment continues to favor a stronger MYR, the pair may be headed lower in the near-term. He tips a the pair to trade in a 3.0320-3.0380 range Tuesday. Maybank IB Research says the pair is likely to trend lower based on charts after it breached the key 3.0775 level, with the next support levels at 3.0200, then 3.0000; and 2.9750 in the longer-term. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0803 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's STI is down 0.5% at 3,195.29, giving up morning gains as sentiment turns negative following a decline in Hong Kong shares. The STI is likely to hold above last week's 3.177.78 low, says AmFraser's Najeeb Jarhom. Separately, Jarhom says that the STI may remain in a tight 50 to 60 point range but a "mini-rally" ahead of the Feb. 18 budget could test 3270-3300 resistance if big cap companies' earnings and budget incentives exceed expectations. "The cue seems to be investors are holding on to positions in anticipation of pleasant surprises on the earnings, GDP and budget fronts, which may not have been fully discounted," he says. Genting Singapore (G13.SG) is the biggest loser, down 3.3% at S$2.06, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Neptune Orient Lines\f28 \uc2 \f2  (N03.SG) sheds 2.2% to S$2.24. Rig builders, however, are holding on to early gains. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Keppel Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (BN4.SG) is up 0.7% at S$11.92, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sembcorp Marine\f28 \uc2 \f2  (S51.SG) is also up 0.7% at S$5.40, while parent \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sembcorp Industries\f28 \uc2 \f2  (U96.SG) is up 1.3% at S$5.29. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0757 GMT [Dow Jones] March bunds are down 0.07 at 122.58, having hit a new contract low of 122.42 earlier Monday. According to WestLB, the downtrend channel of the past few weeks remains firmly in place and the contract is likely to test key support at 122.07, the low on the continuation chart from March 12 last year, sooner rather than later. Resistance is at 122.90 and 123.16. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par +44-20-7842-9464; markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0753 GMT [Dow Jones] March bunds are slightly lower, having hit a new contract low right at the open. Treasurys are also trading weak while equities are expected to open higher as the recent run of better-than-expected economic data supports demand for riskier assets. German manufacturing orders at 1100 GMT are the main economic release of note Monday. March bunds are down 0.05 at 122.60, having hit a new contract low of 122.42.(neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par +44-20-7842-9464; markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0751 GMT [Dow Jones] India's long-end overnight indexed swaps are higher after RBI deputy governor Subir Gokarn said rising crude oil prices due to the political upheaval in Egypt will impact monetary policy decisions, says a trader at a privately-run bank. The five-year OIS rate is at 8.15%/8.17% from 8.08%/8.11% at Friday's close. The one-year OIS rate is at 7.49%/7.50% from 7.46%/7.48% last close. "There is more payment on the long-end tracking hardening bond yields, while short-end rate upside is less because of improving liquidity in banking system." He tips the five-year OIS rate in a 8.15%-8.20% band during rest of the session. The Egypt situation "is going to have an impact on our thinking, (and on) our action going forward," Gokarn said Sunday at a conference. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0746 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR falls to a fresh 13-year low at 3.0360 vs 3.0420 late Wednesday, tracking the USD's weakness vs the EUR. Two dealers say the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is suspected to have been buying dollars at various levels, and most recently at 3.0630, effectively keeping the support level at 3.0630 firm. "Selling hasn't been so aggressive in the afternoon with the presence of Bank Negara," says one dealer, adding that as sentiment continues to favor a stronger MYR, the pair may be headed lower in the near-term. He tips a the pair to trade in a 3.0320-3.0380 range Tuesday. Maybank IB Research says the pair is likely to trend lower based on charts after it breached the key 3.0775 level, with the next support levels at 3.0200, then 3.0000; and 2.9750 in the longer-term. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0723 GMT [Dow Jones] So long as China keeps allowing some CNY appreciation, Treasury will continue to face difficulty slapping the label of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator on Beijing without new legislation to facilitate doing so, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Sean Callow; the U.S. Treasury's semi-annual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  report released Friday declared the CNY "substantially undervalued," but as expected didn't name China a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator. "Tougher action on China is only plausible (and still unlikely) under new legislation, momentum on which has slowed since the November mid-term elections." That, Callow says, is despite the fact that Treasury has added an argument about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on USD/CNY equating to Beijing's borrowing from its public. Meanwhile, the Treasury report takes a tougher line on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . This could matter for the KRW, Callow says, as any further BOK \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "would certainly not win any new votes" for the U.S.-South Korea free trade agreement that needs to be ratified by Congress to take effect. (toko.sekiguchi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0657 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI up 0.3% at 1535.83 in early afternoon trade. A local dealer says buying interest remains strong with investors returning to the market after the Lunar New Year break. Plantation stocks are leading the way on a stronger CPO outlook. "There's quite a bit of institutional buying but regional sentiment is turning a tad negative, so we would be a little cautious... there could be some profit-taking later today," the dealer says, adding that foreign funds are also retruning to the market after the sell-off in late January. The USD/MYR hit a fresh 13-year low at 3.0360 earlier today from 3.0420 late Wednesday. The dealer tips resistance at 1550, with support at 1530. The market breadth is positive with 411 advancers compared with 337 decliners. Among advancers \f28 \uc2 \f2 KL Kepong\f28 \uc2 \f2  (2445.KU) is up 5.2% at MYR22.72, Axiata (6888.KU) is up 1.9% at MYR4.95, and Maybank (1155.KU) is up 0.8% at MYR8.80. Among decliners Genting (3182.KU) is down 3.2% at MYR10.98, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malaysia Marine & Heavy Engineering\f28 \uc2 \f2  (5186.KU) is off 0.8% at MYR6.01. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is down 0.5% at 23,794.14, succumbing to profit-taking as the index's 1.8% spike in half-day of trade Wednesday before the long holiday appears overdone. Market volume is light at HK$40.20 billion and it's still lower than Wednesday's half-day volume, suggesting that profit-taking pressure is not heavy; the 50-day moving average at 23,418 appears quite safe. Everbright Research pegs the HSI's 2011 fair value at 25,800 which represents 14X forward P/E, but it adds that if market sentiment turns more optimistic on potential fund inflow, then the HSI may trade up to 16X P/E, translating into 29,470-point index target. Most blue chips are lower but Esprit (0330.HK) extends gains in afternoon and the stock rises 4.1% to HK$39.30, as investors bet on solid 1H results due Thursday. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi ends +0.5% at 2081.74, but it's well off an intraday high of 2017.83 as recent outperformers including shipbuilders and energy stocks fall on profit-taking. "Investors remain wary amid growing concerns over inflation in emerging markets and possible rate hikes in China and Korea this month," says Bae Sung-young at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hyundai Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 . He adds, if the USD/KRW falls below 1,100, it could hurt export-oriented plays such as technology stocks and auto shares. The USD/KRW fell to 1107.50 vs 1116.90 on Feb 1. "But long-term investors won't leave emerging economies due to their growth potential and revival in consumption," he says. The benchmark index is tipped to trade in a 2050-2180 band this week. Among losers, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  (005930.SE) is down 1.1% at KRW972,000, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\f28 \uc2 \f2  (009540.SE) is down 2.5% at KRW474,000 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 SK Innovation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (096770.SE) is off 3.2% at KRW195,000. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0634 GMT [Dow Jones] The Swiss January CPI is likely to be unchanged to slightly lower, with increases in administrative and oil prices offset by lower clothing prices due to the winter sales, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Of interest for monetary policy will be the domestic components and particularly the sub-series on services prices, making up 58% of the goods basket of Swiss consumers," says economist Reto Huenerwadel. "In light of the above, we expect the inflation differential between the EU area and Switzerland to reach 200 basis points, i.e. a level reached only on two prior occasions in the last 20-odd years," he says. The CPI data are due for release on Feb. 10 at 0815 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0627 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD extends its decline to 7.7826 from 7.7845 earlier Monday, below 7.7884 late Wednesday, as funds flow into Asian markets after the Lunar New Year holiday, says a senior trader at a local bank. "Investors' eyes are on rosy Chinese equities and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when the markets are set to open this Wednesday. The positive sentiment supports the Hong Kong dollar," though a drop in the city's stock market is limiting the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's gain. He tips the pair to trade between 7.7800-7.7850 in the session. (fiona.law@dowjones.com) [ 02-07-11 0303ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110207e7270003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
