\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(11/8) South Korea Report: The Wild Won\b0\par\par\uc2 1,230 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2010\par 22:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par R6\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) \par    By Min-Jeong Lee \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What's the won worth? In South Korea, you just have to look up to find out. The value is visible nearly everywhere, even flashed on electronic signs as people wait for buses and subway trains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The reason for the obsession is simple: The won changes value frequently and sometimes wildly. In part, that's because it's one of the few \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia to float freely. And in part, it's because South Korea's $1 trillion economy depends heavily on international trade, so the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't help but be buffeted by global trade and financial flows.\par \par \uc2 For South Korea, such a free-market approach to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a point of pride: As a developed country, it wants to avoid imposing the kind of strict regulations on the movement of capital that so many less-developed countries practice.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, though, the won's volatility can be a big problem for a country where more than 40% of economic output comes from the value of the goods it exports, 70% of food is imported and hundreds of thousands of students study overseas. It forces companies of all sizes to routinely engage in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging -- and parents to simply guess when to time transfers for overseas tuition payments. And for foreigners operating businesses in the country, the won's volatility can have a big impact on their plans for sending money into the country to open offices, build factories or make payroll.\par \par \uc2 Curt Olson, a former chief executive of the South Korean branch of a major financial company, says accountants at headquarters assigned an exchange rate for the won on which internal incentives were based.\par \par \uc2 "Our actual transactions would reflect the exchange rate, but as a business we were graded on a fixed rate," Mr. Olson says. "That was very internal, a fake number to make sure we didn't do crazy things running the business because of the exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 Some economists and government officials argue the country could fix the problem by putting some -- though not strict -- restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, while others say it would be better to go the opposite way and loosen the existing rules on the won. What's more, South Korean officials are seeking agreement at this week's G-20 summit on a "safety net" that would guarantee assistance to countries that are subject to volatile fund flows and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings. They want to find a way to tame volatility in the won without having to opt for aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  also supports plans by the International Monetary Fund to create new credit lines that would go beyond the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -swap agreements that are now the chief means of stability.\par \par \uc2 "The country is in dire need of the government's efforts to reduce that volatility," says Kim Seung-hyun, an economist with Taurus Investment & Securities.\par \par \uc2 The path of the won in recent years is a testament to the dilemma the country faces. Before the Asian financial crisis in 1997, the won was allowed to appreciate against the dollar only within a certain range designated by the government. Today, many other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain at least partially pegged to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 In order to receive financial assistance from the IMF and global banks in 1997, South Korea had to open its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market so that the won could float against the dollar and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That's when the won became driven by capital flows. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats against the dollar, but the spot market is open for only six hours, which limits trading activity when the local financial markets are shut. Overnight, the won can only be traded via the forward market.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's big companies have adjusted, hiring accountants and advisers who engage in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging to seek some protection from the volatility. Indeed, shipbuilders are the biggest users of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedges. They use them to protect the value of orders because it routinely takes three to four years to deliver a finished ship.\par \par \uc2 But South Korea's smaller companies have a tougher time, even after trying to hedge against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings themselves. Saracom, a midsize maker of marine navigation and communication gear, was delisted from the Kosdaq Stock Market, the smaller of Korea's stock exchanges, early last year after the won's rapid plunge in 2008 left the firm with significant losses from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  options contracts. Saracom declined an interview request.\par \par \uc2 The won's volatility, however, has created the biggest problems for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government.\par \par \uc2 In late 2008, with foreign capital flowing out and the won losing value, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars to try to slow the decline of the won. The result: The bank's foreign reserves fell sharply, low enough to stir worries about the country's ability to cover its short-term borrowings at a time when investors around the world had become extremely averse to any institution or country with a debt problem.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since rebuilt its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and the ratio of short-term foreign-denominated debt to reserves is now 55%, down from 80% during the crisis.\par \par \uc2 But a debate still rages in the country over how to fix the interwoven problems of capital flows and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values.\par \par \uc2 Some economists argue South Korea should take steps toward making the won develop into a more widely exchanged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on international markets by letting it be traded around the clock on the spot market, instead of just six hours a day. Such a move, they say, would allow the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to grow large enough to become more immune to temporary capital flows. The argument goes that if South Korea allowed the won to be exchanged via the spot market all the time, then more people would use the won and capital flows in and out of the country would have less influence on its value.\par \par \uc2 In June, though, the government went in a different direction by placing restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging at companies and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivative trading at banks, a move that critics likened to capital controls. The government was trying to prevent companies from over-hedging, which can be as dangerous as not hedging at all. One of the new rules include a limit on companies (mostly importers and exporters) that prevent them from hedging more than 100% worth of the revenue they're hedging. Before the new rules the ceiling was 125%.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, as the won continued its climb against the U.S. dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  routinely stepped into the market, selling won, to smooth out its rise. Traders say they've come to expect such moves, but not everyone is pleased.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate bodes well when it's predictable, but artificial \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't make the exchange rate stay at a certain level," says Hong Gidon, who until recently was chief financial officer of EMLSI, a semiconductor maker in Jeju. "Rather, it increases market uncertainty, which really doesn't help much."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lee is a reporter for Dow Jones Newswires in Seoul. She can be reached at min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 07, 2010 16:10 ET (21:10 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101107e6b7002kg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
