\par\par \b \uc2 DJ AT A GLANCE: Exchange Rates Dominate G-20 Finance Officials' Meeting\b0\par\par\uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2010\par 03:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 THE EVENT: Finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors from the Group of 20 leading industrialized and emerging nations are gathering in Gyeongju, southeastern Korea, for meetings Friday and Saturday. Dow Jones Newswires' coverage of the event can be found by searching the code N/G20.\par \par \uc2 THE SITUATION: While the meeting is for the ministers and governors to prepare for the G-20 summit in Seoul next month, the issue of exchange rates will likely dominate discussions amid the U.S. dollar's continued weakness, the Chinese yuan's relative strength and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by emerging economies to damp their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' rise.\par \par \uc2 An early draft communique being circulated shows the group aims to work toward a "more market-determined exchange-rate system" and promises to "refrain from competitive undervaluation" of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner said Thursday he plans to use the meetings to advance efforts to "rebalance" the world economy so it is less reliant on U.S. consumers, to move toward establishing "norms" on exchange-rate policy, and to persuade others the U.S. doesn't aim to devalue its way to prosperity.\par \par \uc2 IN THE BACKGROUND: The U.S. has been pressuring China to speed up the appreciation of the yuan, saying it is being kept artificially low to support its exporters, resulting in the U.S. posting a massive trade deficit with the Asian giant. China has maintained, however, that the problem lies with U.S. and its easy money policies, which have weighed on the dollar.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, other emerging nations, notably G-20 members Brazil and \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  have had to repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to slow the rise in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the tide of capital inflows as money from low-yielding developed markets seek higher returns in the less developed world.\par \par \uc2 There is concern that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tensions could escalate into an all-out "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" that leaves no winners.\par \par \uc2 Australian Treasury Secretary Ken Henry Thursday called on major world economies not to retreat to protectionist policies, warning that current weak conditions globally are a likely breeding ground for bad economic policy making.\par \par \uc2 MARKET ACTION: \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  majors are trading in a tight range as investors adopt a cautious approach. The dollar, which rose against its major rivals on Thursday, is steady as traders appear reluctant to push the greenback much higher before the G-20 outcome is known.\par \par \uc2 At around 0100 GMT, the euro was fetching $1.3906 against the dollar, from at $1.3922 late Thursday in New York, and at Y112.96 against the yen, from Y113.22. The dollar was buying Y81.23, compared with Y81.35.\par \par \uc2 Dow Jones' coverage of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  situation also includes: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER NODA: G-20 SHOULD REAFFIRM NEED OF MORE FLEXIBLE EMERGING \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Minister of Finance Yoshihiko Noda said Friday that the Group of 20 must reaffirm the need for more flexibility in emerging countries' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the group's planned two-day meeting starting later in the day. (published 0041 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ GOV SHIRAKAWA: G-20 TO DISCUSS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AMONG OTHER INTERNATIONAL ECONOMIC ISSUES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said Friday he expects officials from the Group of 20 advanced and developing nations to discuss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during their planned two-day meeting starting later in the day. (published 0053 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  =2nd UPDATE: G-20 Seeks To Forge Elusive Consensus On \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  =UPDATE: G-7 To Meet On G-20 Sidelines Friday, Likely To Discuss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  =UPDATE: Australian Treasury Head Says World Should Resist Protectionism \par  =UPDATE: G-20 Likely To Retain \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Reference In Communique - Official \par  =Japan Noda: To Discuss Promotion Of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Flexibility At G-20 \par  =WSJ: Geithner's Goal: Rebalanced World Economy \par  =INTERVIEW: Australia Treasurer Wants Global Move To Market Exchange Rates \par  S Korea Vice Fin Min: Expect Agreement On Easing '\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  War' \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 21, 2010 21:41 ET (01:41 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101022e6am0007j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
