\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/KRW Erases Gains; Drops On Geithner Comments\b0\par\par\uc2 1,557 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2010\par 07:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0547 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW abruptly reverses course and falls to 1,125.1 from 1,132 earlier, vs 1,129.6 late yesterday in Seoul following U.S. Treasury Secretary Geithner's comments that G-20 shouldn't stop undervalued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from rising and that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates should be consistent with fundamentals. His remarks spurring view local authorities won't be able to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as much as they'd like to prevent KRW rise, traders say; BOK suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  frequently in recent months to curb KRW's steep gains. "I think pair could fall to the low-1,120s area," says local bank trader. Adds, "Geithner has weakened local authorities' reasoning for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and has said something that would stop \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the host of the G-20 summit. Now authorities won't be able to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aggressively even if they don't think it's fair." (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0541 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.3% at 992.14 at midday pause in moderate trade. Benchmark weaving in and out of positive territory amid lack of cues. Market still expecting capital inflows to continue buoying sentiment, but concerns Thai authorities may impose additional measures to curb THB rise also capping upside, says Finansia Syrus Securities' analyst Somchai Anektaweepon. Tips 980-1000 range for session. Among top actives, Banpu (BANPU.TH) +2.6% at historical high of THB780, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +4.4% at THB141.50, CP All (CPALL.TH) +3.7% at THB41.75, PTT (PTT.TH) down 0.3% at THB304, \uc2 Berli Jucker\uc2  (BJC.TH) down 1.0% at THB20.10.(piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0533 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares recoup early losses after \uc2 Vinamilk\uc2  (VNM.VH) reports 9-month net profit +62.5% on-year at record VND2.885 trillion, raising hopes more bluechip companies will post solid 3Q results next week. Benchmark index ends flat at 445.21 vs 442.32 intraday low. Still, "concerns over rising inflation and high interest rates remain. Investors will tread with caution ahead of key economic data and policy rate review next week," says Hanoi-based brokerage analyst. Volume thin with 27.8 million shares valued at VND630 billion traded. Among actives, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) ends down 1.3% at VND15,400, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) ends down 1.6% at VND25,300, Meco (MCG.VH) ends down 4.8% at VND18,100. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0527 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures up 0.9% at 9450 in directionless trading as investors await outcome of G-20 meeting and Japanese earnings season next week, market analysts say. "Overall sentiment on the outlook for Japanese corporations is not bad," says Kenichi Hirano, strategist at Tachibana Securities. Says April-September earnings results may boost market, but gains may depend on companies' full-year earnings forecasts. Says futures index may hover near 9500 for time being. Trading volume remains low at under 23,000 contracts vs 25-day moving average of about 65,000. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0524 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Taiwan's September industrial output growth likely +15.3% on-year vs August +23.4% according to Dow Jones poll of 9 economists. Slowing growth due to soft international demand economists say. "The global manufacturing sector has been slowing significantly, but demand seems to be stabilizing and could be picking up steam going into next year," says \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  economist Grace Ng. Adds, industrial output growth likely moderate through end of year. Data due Monday 0800 GMT.(paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0518 GMT [Dow Jones] With all eyes on Wednesday's CPI report in Australia as key to next RBA rate decision, risk is big sell-off in bonds, says Sydney-based interest rates strategist. "Market is pricing 40% change (RBA hikes rates at November meeting), but I'm suggesting it should be 70%-30% or even 80%-20% they hike. So, even if CPI matches expectations, there could be a decent sell off," says strategist. Aussie 3-year recently off 3 ticks at 95.05. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0510 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's NT$40 billion reopened 5-year bond sold at yield of 0.903%, exceeds market estimates by about 1-2 bps, says trader from U.K. bank. "But the bullish trend in the local bond market is still intact, buying interest -- mainly from local funds -- is still very strong." Tips yields on 5-year paper to hover in 0.85%-0.88% range next week, last at 0.8852% vs yesterday's close 0.8740%. Adds, foreign buying of TWD bonds appears less aggressive than weeks ago. 10-year paper not very actively traded this morning; 10-year yield last at 1.228% vs yesterday's close 1.2336%. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0454 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold taking direction from EUR but temptation to take profits after recent rally means magnitude of any downside tends to be greater than EUR suffers, and rebounds relatively weaker in the short term, says Darren Heathcote, head of trading at Investec in Sydney. Spot gold at $1,326.40/oz, up 70 cents after dropping 1.5% overnight. EUR/USD at 1.3926, unchanged from late New York. Adds, G20 meeting of finance ministers, central bankers unlikely to be a big factor for gold with potential for any significant result limited. "There is so much noise out there. I would say it is going to be difficult to negotiate anything meaningful," he says. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0451 GMT [Dow Jones] STI down 0.1% at 3159.63 midday, with two decliners for every gainer; 950 million shares traded worth S$919.8 million; local trader says "the market's quite boring actually," expects sideways trade to remain; technically, says market likely to drift lower next couple of sessions but not by much. Another trader says market lacklustre pre-G20 meeting this weekend, "we may see a little pick up towards the end of the day as we've seen selling throughout most of this week, so there may be a little bit of bargain hunting." New listings dominate most actives; today's debutant Mun Siong (MF6.SG) top traded stock, at S$0.265 vs S$0.20 IPO price, with 106 million shares traded. GLP (MC0.SG) off 1.7% at S$2.25, \uc2 Mapletree Industrial Trust\uc2  (ME8U.SG) down 3.4% at S$1.12. \uc2 Keppel Corp\uc2  (BN4.SG) +0.6% at S$9.77 after rig builder's 3Q beat estimates. Support tipped at 3142, 2-week low. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +0.2% midday at 3594.861 in light volume, with gains in select coal, construction-related stocks keeping index in positive territory, traders say; tip index in 3590-3610 range for rest of day. Still, "profit-taking by local retailers has pulled down the main index from its high," says trader at Valbury Securities. Among gainers: toll road operator \uc2 Jasa Marga\uc2  (JSMR.JK) +4.2% at IDR3,725, coal miner \uc2 Indo Tambangraya\uc2  (ITMG.JK) +7.5% at IDR50,000, while rival \uc2 Harum Energy\uc2  (HRUM.JK) +4.7% at IDR5,550 on hopes for strong 9-month earnings. Profit-taking hits tin miner Timah (TINS.JK), down 2.4% at IDR3,075 after 