ets mostly lower; Nikkei flat, S&P/ASX off 0.8%, Shanghai Composite +0.5%, HSI down 1.0%, STI down 0.1%, KLCI down 0.4%, NZX-50 +0.2%, Kospi off 1.4%, Taiex 1.8% lower, SET slipped 0.3%, Sensex down 1.2%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR fell vs USD and JPY as investors sold common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  following dovish comments from ECB President Trichet over weekend. EUR/USD at 1.3863 vs 1.3974 late New York trade Friday; EUR/JPY fell to 3-week low at 112.49 vs 113.48. USD/JPY at 81.19 vs 81.41. PBOC adviser Li Daokui quoted in China Securities Journal Monday as saying that China can solve CNY exchange rate issue by decreasing its dependence on foreign markets. Australia new motor vehicle sales +0.9% on-month seasonally-adjusted; nation's Treasurer Swan says artificially lowering value of AUD would be dangerous, after \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached parity with USD briefly on Friday for first time since it was floated in 1983. BOK Governor Kim Choong-soo says he expects this year's consumer price inflation for South Korea to be at 2.9%, slightly higher than bank's estimate in July of 2.8%. Singapore September non-oil exports +22.7% on-year vs +30.8% in August, +18.1% expected. NZ 3Q CPI +1.1% on-quarter vs revised +0.2% for 2Q, +1.0% expected. U.K. Rightmove October House Price Index +3.1% on-month vs 1.1% fall in September. New Zealand's annual inflation in 3Q fell to its lowest level since March 2004, giving RBNZ no reason to resume interest rate hikes; 3Q CPI +1.5% on-year, bang in line with median in Dow Jones poll of 10 economists; 3Q CPI +1.1% on-quarter vs +1.0% expected. Spot gold down $8.30 at $1,360.60/oz from NY close on Friday. November Nymex crude oil futures down 62 cents at $80.62/bbl on Globex. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0625 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY likely to stay under pressure this week, even if greenback extends recovery against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as short-term investors sell on view Tokyo is unlikely to conduct USD-buying, JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before meeting later this week of G20 finance ministers, central bankers in Gyeongju, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears out of question this week ahead of the G20, as Japanese authorities would not want to be that provocative," says senior dealer at major Japanese bank. While Finance Minister Noda said Friday Japan would again "take decisive steps when necessary" to curb excessive exchange rate moves, "regardless of whether there are Group of Seven or Group of 20 meetings," dealer says current pace of USD/JPY decline has not been excessive. Pair last 81.16 vs earlier high 81.53.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0621 GMT [Dow Jones] Bullish AUD/USD traders looking to enhance returns in range above parity consider overlaying 3-month 1x2 AUD call/USD put spread, says Dow Jones CommentaryPlus senior analyst Russell Floyd; suggests buying A$10 million Jan 18 0.9985 AUD call/USD put for 192 USD pips, selling A$20 million Jan 18 1.0300 AUD call/USD put for 96 USD pips for zero upfront premium. For those looking to secure profits after strong spot run-up, suggests hedging in range below spot with 1x2 AUD put/USD call spread: buy Jan 18 expiry A$10 million 0.9790 AUD put/USD call for 284 USD pips, sell Jan 18 expiry A$20 million 0.9375 AUD put/USD call for 142 USD pips. See =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  OPTION VIEWS: Consider Aussie Ratio Spreads Around Parity. (murugesan.suppayyan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0620 GMT [Dow Jones] Some time today, four-member panel set up by India's environment ministry on July 28 expected to submit report on South Korean steelmaker Posco's (005490.SE) Orissa steel plant project. Panel investigating whether project will violate rights of local tribes living on forest land likely to be cleared for $12-billion steel plant's construction, adherence to other environmental regulations. Once report submitted to Ministry of Environment and Forests, yet another panel will discuss it, submit recommendations to environment minister, Jairam Ramesh, whose decision will be guided by committee's recommendation. In August, ministry shot down \uc2 Vedanta Resources\uc2 ' (VED.LN) five-year-old proposal to mine for bauxite. Over weekend, local media reported panel split; could make Ramesh decision trickier. Getting environmental approval for projects has always been tough, time-consuming as requires layers of local, state, federal approvals. But analysts feel Posco project may not meet fate of Vedanta's proposal. Too much riding on it. South Korean government has seriously pushed project with Indian government at highest level. See: =WSJ BLOG/India Real Time: A Judgment Day of Sorts for Posco (arlene.chang@wsj.com) [ 10-18-10 0242ET ]\par \par \uc2 0615 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end down 1.8% at 8060.54, weighed by weakness in heavyweight exporters on concerns of strong TWD and HTC's (2498.TW) fall on Apple Daily report, citing an unnamed analyst, as saying new Window Mobile 7 handsets have reboot problems, says Taiwan International Securities assistant vice president Andrew Teng. "We may see more consolidation Tuesday as the market has priced in most of the good news. But Taiex should see firm support at 8000 as long-term economic fundamentals remain solid." HTC off 6.9% at NT$624.00, TSMC (2330.TW) off 2.3% at NT$60.50, UMC (2303.TW) off 2.2% at NT$13.35. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com) [ 10-18-10 0242ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101018e6ai00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
