\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Denmark Deposit Rate May Rise To 1.05%\b0\par\par\uc2 1,920 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2010\par 08:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0640 GMT [Dow Jones] Denmark's Nationalbank said Thursday it would raise its rate of interest for certificates of deposit from Friday by 0.1 percentage points to 0.60% and this may be the first step towards raising the deposit rate to the 1.05% level of the key lending rate, says Jacob Graven at Sydbank. "It is the normal situation that these two rates are the same and only during the crisis, during the last one and half years, have rates on certificates of deposit been lower," says Graven. When the two rates are equal the government could raise the lending rate, Graven says, "of course they could do it before but that is not very likely". European short-term interest rates in the money market remain higher than in Denmark and have driven the euro up against the krone. EUR/DKK trades at 7.4561. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0638 GMT [Dow Jones] Cash bunds are steady Monday, taking cues from overnight gains in Treasurys and generally dovish comments from the ECB's Trichet. Bunds fell Friday, with the long end of the curve tracking Treasurys lower as investors judged the Fed will succeed in pushing inflation higher. Meanwhile, ECB President Trichet over the weekend said a majority of the governing council did not share the view of Bundesbank's Weber that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should stop buying bonds of peripheral euro-zone countries. Weber's comments earlier this month had pressured the markets so the latest news flow should ease worries that the ECB is likely to exit its loose monetary stance pretty soon. There is no economic release of note in the euro zone, although US industrial production data are out. December bunds are down 0.05 at 130.67 and the 10-year bund is up at 98.92, yielding 2.373%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0635 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 2.1% at HK$3.283 billion, 5.5% of total market volume vs 5.2% at Friday's half-day, suggesting bears not stepping up bets despite HSI in 2-day losing streak. ICBC (1398.HK) leads with HJK$441.74 million, followed by A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) with HK$271.81 million. Next come \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) with HK$228.58 million, Hutchison (0013.HK) with HK$220.43 million, HKEx (0388.HK) with HK$177.81 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0635 GMT [Dow Jones] Switzerland's economy is performing well, compared with its European neighbors, particularly in the labor market, according to \uc2 McKinsey & Co\uc2 . "While Switzerland wasn't directly covered in the EU-15-focused report, it's deeply linked to the European economy," it says. "In terms of the reform agenda laid out, Switzerland shows very good performance in the labor market, with one of the lowest unemployment rates in Europe," it notes. "The employment rate of older workers was a very high 68 percent, and it also scores well on innovation metrics like triadic patents per capita, outperforming Germany in 2007 by about 50%," it says. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] Much-awaited opening of Mumbai's new diamond trading hub, Bharat Diamond Bourse, finally happened Sunday. Building $250 million trading hub took 18 years and twice the amount of money expected after repeated delays due to everything from structural modifications to battles between leading diamond merchants to flooding from torrential rains. Anoop Mehta, president of bourse, said exchange, which has 2,500 offices, four walk-in vaults, 24,500 safe deposit boxes and a 6,200 sq ft trading floor, is world's largest. He said nearly $18 billion worth of diamonds exported from India during last fiscal year; he is hoping new trading hub will bring more international diamond traders to India's financial capital. "We haven't had foreigners coming here for trading and manufacturing of their diamonds because we just didn't have infrastructure that was at par with Israel or Antwerp," Mehta said. "Now that we have the facilities, I am certain that more diamond traders will be encouraged to set up offices in India." See: =WSJ BLOG/India Real Time: Mumbai's New Diamond Market Opens at Last (arlene.chang@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0628 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower; Nikkei flat, S&P/ASX off 0.8%, Shanghai Composite +0.5%, HSI down 1.0%, STI down 0.1%, KLCI down 0.4%, NZX-50 +0.2%, Kospi off 1.4%, Taiex 1.8% lower, SET slipped 0.3%, Sensex down 1.2%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR fell vs USD and JPY as investors sold common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  following dovish comments from ECB President Trichet over weekend. EUR/USD at 1.3863 vs 1.3974 late New York trade Friday; EUR/JPY fell to 3-week low at 112.49 vs 113.48. USD/JPY at 81.19 vs 81.41. PBOC adviser Li Daokui quoted in China Securities Journal Monday as saying that China can solve CNY exchange rate issue by decreasing its dependence on foreign markets. Australia new motor vehicle sales +0.9% on-month seasonally-adjusted; nation's Treasurer Swan says artificially lowering value of AUD would be dangerous, after \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached parity with USD briefly on Friday for first time since it was floated in 1983. BOK Governor Kim Choong-soo says he expects this year's consumer price inflation for South Korea to be at 2.9%, slightly higher than bank's estimate in July of 2.8%. Singapore September non-oil exports +22.7% on-year vs +30.8% in August, +18.1% expected. NZ 3Q CPI +1.1% on-quarter vs revised +0.2% for 2Q, +1.0% expected. U.K. Rightmove October House Price Index +3.1% on-month vs 1.1% fall in September. New Zealand's annual inflation in 3Q fell to its lowest level since March 2004, giving RBNZ no reason to resume interest rate hikes; 3Q CPI +1.5% on-year, bang in line with median in Dow Jones poll of 10 economists; 3Q CPI +1.1% on-quarter vs +1.0% expected. Spot gold down $8.30 at $1,360.60/oz from NY close on Friday. November Nymex crude oil futures down 62 cents at $80.62/bbl on Globex. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0625 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY likely to stay under pressure this week, even if greenback extends recovery against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as short-term investors sell on view Tokyo is unlikely to conduct USD-buying, JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before meeting later this week of G20 finance ministers, central bankers in Gyeongju, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears out of question this week ahead of the G20, as Japanese authorities would not want to be that provocative," says senior dealer at major Japanese bank. While Finance Minister Noda said Friday Japan would again "take decisive steps when necessary" to curb excessive exchange rate moves, "regardless of whether there are Group of Seven or Group of 20 meetings," dealer says current pace of USD/JPY decline has not been excessive. Pair last 81.16 vs earlier high 81.53.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0621 GMT [Dow Jones] Bullish AUD/USD traders looking to enhance returns in range above parity consider overlaying 3-month 1x2 AUD call/USD put spread, says Dow Jones CommentaryPlus senior analyst Russell Floyd; suggests buying A$10 million Jan 18 0.9985 AUD call/USD put for 192 USD pips, selling A$20 million Jan 18 1.0300 AUD call/USD put for 96 USD pips for zero upfront premium. For those looking to secure profits after strong spot run-up, suggests hedging in range below spot with 1x2 AUD put/USD call spread: buy Jan 18 expiry A$10 million 0.9790 AUD put/USD call for 284 USD pips, sell Jan 18 expiry A$20 million 0.9375 AUD put/USD call for 142 USD pips. See =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  OPTION VIEWS: Consider Aussie Ratio Spreads Around Parity. (murugesan.suppayyan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0620 GMT [Dow Jones] Some time today, four-member panel set up by India's environment ministry on July 28 expected to submit report on South Korean steelmaker Posco's (005490.SE) Orissa steel plant project. Panel investigating whether project will violate rights of local tribes living on forest land likely to be cleared for $12-billion steel plant's construction, adherence to other environmental regulations. Once report submitted to Ministry of Environment and Forests, yet another panel will discuss it, submit recommendations to environment minister, Jairam Ramesh, whose decision will be guided by committee's recommendation. In August, ministry shot down \uc2 Vedanta Resources\uc2 ' (VED.LN) five-year-old proposal to mine for bauxite. Over weekend, local media reported panel split; could make Ramesh decision trickier. Getting environmental approval for projects has always been tough, time-consuming as requires layers of local, state, federal approvals. But analysts feel Posco project may not meet fate of Vedanta's proposal. Too much riding on it. South Korean government has seriously pushed project with Indian government at highest level. See: =WSJ BLOG/India Real Time: A Judgment Day of Sorts for Posco (arlene.chang@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0615 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end down 1.8% at 8060.54, weighed by weakness in heavyweight exporters on concerns of strong TWD and HTC's (2498.TW) fall on Apple Daily report, citing an unnamed analyst, as saying new Window Mobile 7 handsets have reboot problems, says Taiwan International Securities assistant vice president Andrew Teng. "We may see more consolidation Tuesday as the market has priced in most of the good news. But Taiex should see firm support at 8000 as long-term economic fundamentals remain solid." HTC off 6.9% at NT$624.00, TSMC (2330.TW) off 2.3% at NT$60.50, UMC (2303.TW) off 2.2% at NT$13.35. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 UBS\uc2  is bullish on the weekly outlook for EUR/CHF and sees upside potential to 1.3665 should 1.3494 be breached. On the downside, there is strong technical support at 1.3265 and then at 1.3027, according to \uc2 UBS\uc2  technical analyst Gareth Berry. EUR/CHF at 1.3359 from a previous close at 1.3396. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com) [ 10-18-10 0240ET ]\par \par \uc2 0606 GMT [Dow Jones] China government will do another serious \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if housing prices rise more than 1% on-month for several consecutive months, says CLSA strategist Andy Rothman. "As long as prices rise in line with nominal urban income - about 1% on-month - the government will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with dramatic measures, but we should expect continued efforts to cool speculative buying, which accounts for about 16% of total sales," he says. Adds, over last 5 years, average annual nominal urban income +13% while average house prices +12% per year. Official data shows China property prices +0.5% on-month in September. Rothman says China housing prices unlikely to rise too fast in near future as housing investment, starts and completions all rose rapidly in September, suggesting supply-demand balance will remain healthy. (esther.fung@dowjones.com) [ 10-18-10 0240ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101018e6ai00043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
