\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(10/15) Japan, Korea Tussle Over  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,014 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 20:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) \par    By Evan Ramstad \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL -- Japan's decision to criticize \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  threw a spotlight on a little-noticed reason for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economic success this year -- the Korean won's weakness compared with the Japanese yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Like the yen, the won has been rising against the U.S. dollar in recent months. But the lingering difference between the yen and the won helps South Korean companies maintain an edge in pricing and profit margins in the industries in which they compete with Japanese rivals, including automobiles, shipbuilding, steel and electronics.\par \par \uc2 The tussle between South Korea and Japan is part of the broader \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil shaking up governments and businesses across the world. Also Thursday, the Singaporean dollar soared after the city-state tightened monetary policy. U.S. dollar weakness also sent the Australian dollar to a new 28-year high, while the Swiss franc rose to a record against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the euro climbed above $1.41.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar has fallen 12.8% so far this year against the yen but is down only 4.8% against the won. The two traded nearly evenly against the U.S. dollar in 2007 but their performance diverged sharply in 2008 as investors worried about the global economy dove into \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  considered safe, like the yen, while moving out of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  considered more speculative, like the won. The gap reached its widest point in January 2009, but is now almost as wide.\par \par \uc2 That makes Japanese products more expensive than Korean products when translated into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Because most companies choose to adjust prices to compete, the impact is usually seen on the bottom line.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at an event in Tokyo last month, \uc2 Sharp Corp\uc2 . President \uc2 Mikio Katayama\uc2  said the yen's strength and Japan's corporate-tax rate put the company at a disadvantage to Korean competitors. "It's unfair competition, like carrying dumbbells tied to your arms and legs," he said.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Prime Minister Naoto Kan and Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda for the first time blamed South Korea along with China for creating artificially low \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, citing the Bank of Korea's near-routine \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 "I want \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  and China to take responsible actions within common rules," Mr. Kan said, though he cited Japan's own recent market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bring down the yen.\par \par \uc2 In Seoul on Thursday, the Bank of Korea and key government economic agencies criticized Japanese leaders for making inappropriate remarks but declined to talk about their substance. In the past, Korean officials have said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are intended to smooth the direction of the won's value, not change it.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts expect both the won and yen to continue gaining strength against the U.S. dollar in the months ahead, though the gap in relative values may tighten and reduce some of the advantage Korean exporters hold.\par \par \uc2 The exchange-rate issue spilled into Thursday's decision by the BOK's monetary policy committee to leave interest rates unchanged. Many analysts speculated the bank's policy makers worried that higher interest rates would attract capital flows that would further drive up the won's value. BOK Gov. Kim Choong-soo said the won's value was one of many factors influencing the decision to hold the interest rate steady.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, Korean businesses and government officials have worried about the won's gains against the U.S. dollar, which have brought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearly back to where it was in early September 2008 just before the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  triggered a global credit crunch and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation in many countries.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the won's relative advantage against the yen has actually gotten better -- helping Korean exporters along the way. That has meant, for instance, that South Korea's \uc2 Hyundai Motor Corp\uc2 . vehicles have a pricing advantage of 10% to 15% on Japanese manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 For Sung Min Electronics Co., which makes switches and lights for electronics products, the won's advantage against the yen has paid off in Germany, where sales are up 20% compared with last year, says Lim Hyuk , a sales manager. "I think our products have better price competitiveness than Japanese ones," he said.\par \par \uc2 Having a pricing edge against a main competitor is especially important to South Korea because exports account for 43% of its economy, the most of any developed nation, according to the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development. And its export strength is the main reason South Korea's overall economic growth is expected to be around 6% this year, well ahead of Japan's expected 2%.\par \par \uc2 "Japanese firms are struggling with the strong yen against the dollar, but what they have been telling us is the strong yen against the Korean won is much worse," said Minori Uchida, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ. "There are so many cases that Japanese firms lose in infrastructure project auctions overseas, such as India, against Korean rivals and the firms say the exchange rate is one of the big factors."\par \par \uc2 Mitsubishi Motors Corp\uc2 . President \uc2 Osamu Masuko\uc2 , speaking at news conference on electric cars Thursday, faced questions about the won's advantage over the yen. "It is not just an issue of won," he said. "Governments have a common idea now to get their economies back on the recovery trend by weakening their countries' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost exports, but it seems like Japan is lagging behind."\par \par \uc2 Today's competitive situation between South Korea and Japan is a sharp contrast to 2007, when the won was occasionally stronger than the yen against the U.S. dollar. Executives from South Korean manufacturers complained for much of that year about being at a price and profit disadvantage to Japanese rivals.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Juro Osawa, Takashi Mochizuki and Yoshio Takahashi in Tokyo and Jaeyeon Woo in Seoul contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 14, 2010 14:23 ET (18:23 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101014e6ae004iw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
