\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL ECONOMY- \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  tensions persist as markets await Fed\b0\par\par\uc2 902 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2010\par 00:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * China slams U.S., Japan over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance\par \par \uc2  * All eyes on Bernanke, U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report\par \par \uc2  * Verbal jousting mars run-up to key G20 meetings\par \par \uc2  * ECB policymakers demand more emerging mkt flexibility\par \par \uc2  By Aileen Wang and Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2  BEIJING/TOKYO, Oct 15 (Reuters) - Recriminations over \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  reverberated worldwide on Friday ahead of a speech by Federal Reserve chief Ben Bernanke, whose loose policies are blamed by China and others for destabilising capital flows.\par \par \uc2  Beijing kept up the heat on the United States, saying Washington should not make China a scapegoat for its own problems by constantly pressing for a swifter rise in the yuan.\par \par \uc2  Speaking hours before the U.S. Treasury Department is due to deliver its semi-annual assessment of whether China manipulates its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Commerce Ministry spokesman Yao Jian said it was not fair to criticise Beijing's exchange rate policy simply by pointing to China's trade surplus.\par \par \uc2  "Other countries have no right to comment on what is a reasonable level for a country's trade surplus," Yao told a monthly news conference.\par \par \uc2  Sniping over what exchange rates are appropriate to put the world economy back on course is intensifying ahead of a pair meetings of the Group of 20 leading economies in South Korea.\par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymaker Juergen Stark was reported as saying countries must avoid a 'fatal' race to devalue and instead coordinate better to smooth out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings.\par \par \uc2  Both he and his colleague, Jose Manuel Gonzalez-Paramo, said China, which is resisting pressure from the United States and other developed nations to allow its yuan to revalue faster, needed to be more flexible.\par \par \uc2  G20 Finance ministers meet next week in Gyeongju to prepare for a summit of their leaders in Seoul on Nov. 11-12.\par \par \uc2  U.S. data on Thursday showed America's trade deficit with China swelled to a record high of $28 billion in August -- grist to the mill of U.S. politicians who say that China keeps the yuan artificially cheap to help its exporters.\par \par \uc2  Spot yuan ended at its highest closing level against the dollar since its landmark revaluation in July 2005  but U.S. critics say it is still 20 percent or more undervalued.\par \par \uc2  LOGGERHEADS\par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda stressed that countries must work together to strengthen the global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  order. Governments would find it tough to cope with the current environment on their own, he told reporters.\par \par \uc2  Noda struck a more conciliatory tone than on Wednesday, when he criticised \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly to curb the won, and China, for dragging its heels in letting the yuan rise.\par \par \uc2  "Before saying this or that about other countries' \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, we must do what we should do," Noda said.\par \par \uc2  Yao, the Chinese Commerce Ministry spokesman, was in no mood to forgive or forget. Japan has "no reason, no grounds and is not qualified" to criticise China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, he said.\par \par \uc2  Some South Korean newspapers weighed in too, saying Japan risked sacrificing friendly diplomatic relations with its neighbours to placate its export companies, which are suffering due to the yen's rise to a 15-year high against the dollar.\par \par \uc2  The dollar hovered near a 10-month low versus a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket as the conviction hardened that the Fed will further ease monetary policy next month to try to kick-start the lethargic U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2  WAITING FOR BERNANKE\par \par \uc2  Financial markets, which expect the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to begin soon a second round of buying of U.S. Treasury bonds with new money -- to the tune of $500 billion or more -- will hang on Bernanke's words for clues about the likely scope.\par \par \uc2  Bernanke is due to speak at 1215 GMT.\par \par \uc2  The Fed's policy remit is domestic, but the side-effects of minting new dollars to pay for the asset purchases are a weaker dollar and investors' rush into emerging markets, where growth prospects are brighter and yields are higher.\par \par \uc2  In response, several governments have stepped into \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets or tried to curb capital inflows, raising fears of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "race to the bottom" that may trigger protectionism and hit global growth.\par \par \uc2  "With the recovery slow in the advanced countries, each country relies more on exports for growth and tension surrounding \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates is intensifying," South Korean Finance Minister Yoon Jeung-hyun said. "There are signs that this could develop into trade protectionism."\par \par \uc2  Lawrence Summers, a top economic adviser to U.S. President Barack Obama, also flagged protectionism as a rising threat to the global economy that destroys value.\par \par \uc2  Singapore decided on Thursday not stand in the way of market forces and said it would let its dollar rise more quickly.\par \par \uc2  But India \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper a rising rupee, while \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  tried to deter unwanted inflows by keeping interest rates steady even at the risk of allowing inflation to build.\par \par \uc2  Thailand this week imposed a withholding tax on foreign purchases of government bonds. But the new chief of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Bangkok would be wary about further steps.\par \par \uc2  "Stringent measures will have side effects, so what to use and when, we have to look at the real situation," \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  Governor Prasarn Trairatvorakul said.  (Writing by Alan Wheatley and Mike Peacock; Editing by Toby Chopra)  \par \par \uc2 GLOBAL-ECONOMY/ (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101014e6ae00066\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
