\par\par \b \uc2 Japan PM Suggests Further  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  As Yen Touches Highs-Kyodo\b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 15:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Naoto Kan said Thursday that Japan could take action to address excessive volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and prevent a further sharp rise of the yen, which has reached levels against the U.S. dollar unseen in over 15 years, Kyodo News reported.\par \par \uc2 Kan suggested Japan would again step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market following its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sept. 15, the country's first in over six years. But it is widely seen as increasingly difficult for Tokyo to take such a step as some emerging economies have come under pressure to stop any moves to manipulate exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 "We still believe that rapidly changing exchange rates are not desirable," Kan told reporters after the U.S. dollar briefly traded in the Y80 range for the first time since April 1995. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's all-time high of Y79.75 against the dollar was marked on April 19, 1995.\par \par \uc2 "Amid a big shift like this, we need various options, and I have said that when necessary, we will take decisive action," he said.\par \par \uc2 The yen's strength adversely affects the earnings of Japanese exporters and is believed to be slowing the nation's economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Kan's comments came a day after he called on China and South Korea to "take responsible actions under common rules" in reference to their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. He told a Diet committee that efforts made by individual countries to depress their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  never fall within the principles of cooperation among the Group of 20 leading economies.\par \par \uc2 The G-20, which groups both major developed and developing economies, is to hold a summit in Seoul next month with possible cooperation on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy high on the agenda.\par \par \uc2 China has come under growing pressure from the U.S. and other advanced economies to stop maintaining the yuan at a weak level to protect exports.\par \par \uc2 The Group of Seven industrialized economies agreed last week that emerging economies with huge current account surpluses should implement reforms for a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is widely seen as actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the won's appreciation.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which will host the Nov. 11-12 G-20 summit, rejected Kan's comments, lodging a protest with Japan, and Tokyo pledged not to make further "improper" comments regarding Seoul's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, Yonhap News Agency reported Thursday, citing South Korean finance ministry officials.\par \par \uc2 Kan has admitted it is difficult for him to comment on other countries' \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies given Japan's own \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Meanwhile, Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda has said Japan's action was intended to ease the yen's excessive volatility rather than to lead the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a certain level.\par \par \uc2 The G-7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors, who met Oct. 8 in Washington, reconfirmed that disorderly movements in exchange rates have adverse implications for economic and financial stability, Noda said.\par \par \uc2 -Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2900 [ 10-14-10 0941ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020101014e6ae0007w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
