\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:USD/KRW Sustains Marginal Fall; 1,139-1,143 Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,756 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2010\par 07:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0541 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW sustains marginal losses in afternoon trade at 1,141.0 vs 1,142.0 late yesterday on persistent exporter USD selling. But pair little moved from morning levels amid lack of momentum to push pair further down, while euro weakens in Asian trade, failing to give local unit extra boost, traders say. "There's quite a lot of dollar selling from exporters, but given the likelihood of authorities' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (to buy dollars) at levels below KRW1,140, the pair will likely hover around current levels for rest of the session," says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,139-1,143 range. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0535 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares by midday +0.3% at 3055.011 in light volume, off earlier low of 3489.812, led by buying in select resources, automotive stocks on hopes for strong 3Q earnings, traders say; tip resistance at 3520. "I think the main index is set to close at another fresh record high in a generally cautious trend," says fund manager at state-owned pension fund. Among gainers: tin miner Timah (TINS.JK) +5.3% at IDR3,000, gold, nickel miner Antam (ANTM.JK) +3.3% at IDR2,350. Profit-taking hits toll road builder, operator \uc2 Jasa Marga\uc2  (JSMR.JK), down 3.0% at IDR3,200. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0510 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi turns lower, down 0.2% at 1862.91 as investors taking breather before 3Q earnings season; "foreigners are buying KRW220 billion worth of domestic stocks and it's keeping the main index from falling further," says Lee Kyoung-min at \uc2 Woori Investment & Securities\uc2 . Says if major companies come up with strong 3Q results next month, Kospi may get hefty boost to test 1900 level; 1855-1870 band tipped for today. Stocks helping to limit Kospi's fall include technology, shipbuilding, transport shares. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) +0.7% at KRW777,000, \uc2 Hynix Semiconductor\uc2  (000660.SE) +1.1% at KRW22,100, Hyundai Mipo Dockyard (010620.SE) +1.9% at KRW188,500, \uc2 Korean Air Lines\uc2  (003490.SE) +1.8% at KRW77,400. Some shares falling after recent gains: \uc2 Hyundai Engineering & Construction\uc2  (000720.SE) down 2.5% at KRW71,800, SK Energy (096770.SE) off 2.3% at KRW148,500, \uc2 LG Chem\uc2  (051910.SE) down 1.7% at KRW327,000. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0502 GMT [Dow Jones] China likely to continue using targeted measures such as Wednesday's property curbs to rein in excesses in certain parts of the economy, rather than turning to broad-based tightening such as interest rate hikes; Wednesday's measures only the latest example of this strong policy preference. Consumer prices rose 3.5% in August, but mostly on one-off factors such as higher food prices, and economists almost universally expect inflation to ease in coming months. Inflation rates were also much higher during last round of interest rate hikes, over 6% in 2H 2007 and as high as 8.7% in February of 2008. See: =MONEY TALKS: China To Focus On Property, Not Broad Tightening. (aaron.back@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0501 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures down 1.3% at 9440, extending morning losses on concerns about firmer yen (USD/JPY at 83.52 and EUR/JPY at 113.59). "In addition to the stronger yen, the decline in banking shares to fresh year-to-date lows is souring overall sentiment," says Hideki Horikawa, chief analyst at Himawari Securities. MUFG (8306.TO) down 4.0% at Y388 and SMFG (8316.TO) off 3.9% at Y2,432; both hit fresh 2010 lows on concerns over consumer lender affiliates and renewed European debt worries. Puts successive futures index near-term support levels at 9400, then 9200. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0447 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.2% at 1,464.08 midday in range-bound morning session as investors grope for leads from global markets amid dearth of local cues; index caught in tight 6 point range through morning session; Dealer says markets likely to continue to consolidate through rest of session; tips 1,460-1,467 range for rest of session. Immediate resistance at recent high of 1,479.59, with support at Friday low of 1,445.33. Market breadth negative with 348 decliners vs 253 advancers. Advancers led by property, construction stocks with \uc2 Kimlun Corp\uc2 . (5171.KU) +1.9% at MYR1.09 on MYR70 million contract win from \uc2 Malaysian Resources\uc2  (1651.KU), which +1.4% at MYR2.19; \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2 . (2194.KU) +2.0% at MYR3.00. Decliners led by Unisem (5005.KU) down 2.0% at MYR1.98, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2 . (1961.KU) down 1.1% at MYR5.49. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY briefly falls below 83.50, lowest level since Sept. 15, when Japan's government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, amid persistent speculation Fed likely to ease monetary policy further soon, says Yuzo Sakai, senior dealer at Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  & Ueda Harlow; adds, selling by Japan's exporters also pushing pair lower. But notes "if the dollar extends falls against the yen, expectations could grow that the government will step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again to sell the yen, which could discourage investors from actively buying" JPY. Tips support at 83.20 for now; last 83.51 vs session high of 83.81. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0441 GMT [Dow Jones] Action in Singapore stock market notably subdued as investors generally 