\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Central Banks Keep Up  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  As Dollar Weakens\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Roman   \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \uc2 653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 07:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The central banks of \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Singapore, Thailand and Indonesia were suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange markets Wednesday to contain gains in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but analysts say the continued weakness of the U.S. dollar means Asian units should extend their rallies.\par \par \uc2 The wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has continued across the region for most of this year, hasn't been strong enough to reverse the strengthening trend in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as many policymakers are allowing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise slowly to limit imported inflation.\par \par \uc2 That stance was reinforced after China, the top trading partner for key Asian countries, said in June it would let the yuan strengthen against the dollar -- meaning that other Asian countries can maintain their export competitiveness against China even if their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise a bit.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday the Thai baht and the Malaysian ringgit hit their highest levels since the 1997 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis, while the Singapore dollar rose to an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 In a note to investors, Standard Chartered analysts said global dollar weakness, closely tied to market expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve may launch another round of quantitative easing by boosting purchases of U.S. securities, is likely to keep Asian and other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in strong demand. In recent days, the central banks of countries ranging from Russia to Colombia have been suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets because of dollar weakness.\par \par \uc2 "The prospect of another round of quantitative easing by the Fed in the fourth quarter of 2010 should be seen as broadly negative for the dollar," StanChart said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday was particularly significant in Indonesia, which has attracted a surge of inflows thanks to its solid economic performance and high interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Jakarta-based traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of buying around $50 million at IDR8,950 in early trade, and then an unspecified amount at IDR8,945. Neither move managed to send the rupiah lower, however, and the dollar last traded at IDR8,935.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar is still under pressure against the rupiah, as investors' appetite for riskier assets is improving," one dealer in Singapore said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank Indonesia has continued for the past two years, but is now likely at its highest volume ever. Bank Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose 9% in the first three weeks of September, a huge amount even by East Asian standards, mostly due to dollar-buying in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore also were suspected of early \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves Wednesday. In Bangkok, traders said the BOT was spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at THB30.50 against the dollar, but the baht rose further and last traded at THB30.46.\par \par \uc2 In Singapore, the MAS managed to weaken the local dollar slightly after it soared to an all-time high at S$1.3152 against the greenback. The Singapore dollar last traded at S$1.3170.\par \par \uc2 Seoul traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Korea was relatively mild, as the Korean won is still some distance away from two-year highs hit earlier this year. The dollar last traded at KRW1,141, having fallen as low as KRW1,139 earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 (David Roman, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reporter, has been a Dow Jones correspondent since 2002, covering economic and corporate news. He can be reached at 65 6415 4045 or by e-mail: david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 29-09-10 0555GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1184 4253   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100929e69t000cy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
