\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE: Korea Vows More Active  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  If Needed\b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 2010\par 11:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds market moves, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  details.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Kanga Kong \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday it is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more actively in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if needed, a day after making a massive defense of the won, which had been battered by tensions over North Korea.\par \par \uc2 "We will, in close cooperation with the Bank of Korea, act more aggressively to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if it is biased to one side on excessive sentimental unrest," the finance ministry said in a statement after an emergency meeting with the Bank of Korea, the financial-market regulator and the Ministry of Knowledge Economy.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought won for the first time in more than a year Tuesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a nearly one-year low. The BOK sold $2 billion to $2.5 billion for won, traders estimated, in a bid to brake this month's 13% plunge by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Korea's tumbling markets helped unnerve global investors as North Korea's saber-rattling compounded concerns over Europe's debt crisis. Tensions have risen since Seoul last week accused Pyongyang of torpedoing the South Korean warship Cheonan near a disputed maritime border March 26, killing 46.\par \par \uc2 A group of defectors from the North said Tuesday that Pyongyang, which denies involvement in the sinking, last week ordered its citizens and troops to be ready for combat. The North further ratcheted up tensions late Tuesday by announcing it would "totally freeze" relations with the South and pull out of a bilateral nonaggression pact.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's huge won-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 --which accounted for as much as 20% of the day's heavy trading volume--pushed the dollar down sharply from KRW1,277.0, its highest since July 2009. The won weakened a bit Wednesday, with the dollar edging back up to KRW1,253.30 late in the day from KRW1,250.00 late Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Seoul's main stock index rose 1.4% Wednesday, despite continued selling by foreign investors, recouping some of Tuesday's 2.7% loss.\par \par \uc2 "Overall, we can't say that the market has stabilized yet," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a local bank. "It'll probably take some time for that."\par \par \uc2 In its bid to reassure the markets, the government said Wednesday it is ready to take additional measures, such as providing efficient foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity if deemed necessary for market stability.\par \par \uc2 It attributed Tuesday's won plunge partly to a market overreaction, saying the Korean economy is strong enough to weather news from the North with its sound finances, sizable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and a current-account surplus.\par \par \uc2 -By Kanga Kong, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-3700-1900; kanga.kong@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 26, 2010 05:43 ET (09:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100526e65q00210\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
