vestors to set up regional headquarters there, including some corporate tax waivers. Spot gold at $1,191.30/oz, down $1.00 vs NY close. July Nymex crude oil futures down $1.31 at $68.90/bbl. (leslie.shaffer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0624 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP advances to six-month high, buoyed by broad USD strength amid mounting concerns on Europe's debt woes, prompting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper USD rise; pair last 46.99, highest since November 24, from 46.56 late Monday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected to have sold around $400 million from PHP45.95 level, say local bank traders. "It wouldn't be good for us if we breach the 47.00 level, it might set a panic mode," notes one trader. Tips next resistance 47.20 if 47.00 level is breached, resistance 46.80. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 9.8% at HK$2.283 billion at interval, but percentage of market total slightly higher at 6.9%, vs 6.6% Monday's half-day, suggests sentiment remains bearish after HSI down 2.4% midday, looks set to approach psychological 19,000 level soon. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) leads with HK$291.80 million, then Tencent (0700.HK) with HK$174.16 million, China Life (2628.HK) with HK$161.86 million, China Mobile (0941.HK) with HK$146.93 million and \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$93.22 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0613 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex extends loss, now down 1.8% at 16,166.66 tracking similar losses in other Asian markets as euro-zone troubles stoke caution; index hit session-low of 16,123.75 earlier--lowest intraday level in over 3 months; 27 of 30 index stocks down with financials, autos, cement, metal stocks among leading losers. S&P CNX Nifty down 1.9% at 4850.50. "We are seeing an intermediate correction because at the moment, global factors are overpowering investor sentiment," says D.D. Sharma, senior vice-president of research at Anand Rathi Securities; adds, "I would say commodities like sugar and metals are sectors to avoid for now." However, tips good support for Nifty at 4800, and says "in any case, even if we fall more, I don't think the Nifty would slip below 4600." Reliance Communications (532712.BY) down 6.3% at INR138.65, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) down 2.5% at INR811, \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) down 2.5% at INR688.90, \uc2 Grasim Industries\uc2  (500300.BY) down 4.7% at INR2,280. (shikhar.balwani@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0607 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY crosses such as EUR/JPY keep falling across the board as Asia, Tokyo shares extend declines, prompting investors to let go of risky \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says trader at Tokyo bank; EUR/JPY may fall to 110.00, USD/JPY likely to drop to 89.50 in tandem; "fears about the European financial system are lingering and the geopolitical risk in Korea is growing - many players are clearly eager to buy back safe-haven assets," such as JPY. Adds, "as long as stock markets remain volatile, the yen will likely continue to gain favor." EUR/JPY now at 110.34 vs session high of 111.62; USD/JPY at 89.75 vs 90.31. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0606 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds end higher, boosted by brokerage houses buying on Taiex's 3.2% loss, lingering global uncertainties such as political tensions on Korean peninsula, euro zone debt problem, says local bank trader. Tips 5-year bond to rise more short term as "the gap between current 5-year yield and record low yield is around 25 basis points, but the gap between current 10-year yield and record low is only around 5 basis points," he says. 5-year yield ends at 2-month low 0.9531% vs 0.9668% yesterday. 10-year yield ends at 1.3505% vs 1.3832% yesterday, lowest since Oct 8 2009. New 10-year reopened bonds starts when-issued trade at 1.3755% today, ending at 1.3570%, yield tipped to fall in 1.3400%-1.3600% band Wednesday. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0603 GMT [Dow Jones] India overnight call rate off highs, now flat at 3.95% after touching day's high at 4.20% earlier amid heavy borrowing as banks build up cash reserves for forthcoming payout for 3G spectrum auction, says dealer with state-run bank. Tips call rates to move lower to 3.75%-3.85% after liquidity adjustment facility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  closes for day as banks will start lending in overnight market. But dealer notes, "the trend for call rates is higher as the 3G payments will be followed by advance tax outflows which will keep liquidity under pressure." Notes, bulk of outflow for 3G from Friday to push call rates up to 4.40%-4.50% band. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0558 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend losses midday, taking cues from neighbouring markets' declines, led by property developers on speculations of imminent launch of real-estate tax. Shanghai Composite Index last off 2.0% at 2619.05, may test support at 2,600 soon. Internet portal Sina.com reported yesterday Shanghai may include real-estate tax into rules that it's drafting to curb speculative investment in property market. "It's still unclear what the rules may be, but people believe that China will keep its tightening stance on the property market," says Zhang Yuheng at Capital Securities. Property developer \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ) down 3.8% at CNY7.41, Poly Real Estate (600048.SH) down 4.7% at CNY11.58. Shenzhen Composite Index last down 1.5% at 1035.64. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0553 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR again starts falling on growing concerns that euro-zone's sovereign debt turmoil has started to undermine health of region's financial system, may extend declines to 1.2000 vs USD, 105.00 vs JPY in near term, says Masanobu Ishikawa, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Ueda Harlow. Notes many players have become worried over whether euro-zone's faltering financial system could drag down real economy in area; "it's extremely difficult to expect when the euro will hit bottom. Players remain very eager to sell the euro as economic conditions in Europe may worsen down the road." EUR/USD now at 1.2285 vs session high of 1.2372; EUR/JPY at 110.45 vs 111.62. Some traders say Europe investors just entering market, starting to sell EUR. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0552 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW off 1,277.0 high on another round of suspected USD selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to 3-Seoul based traders; but pair still trading higher at 1,259.0 vs 1,214.5 late yesterday in Seoul on concerns over tensions between North, South Korea. "It's a crazy market that (I) haven't seen in a while," says local bank trader. Adds, pair still biased upward despite "the aggressive (dollar) selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Traders decline to provide near term USD/KRW. Pair has traded in wide 1,224-1,277 range so far today. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0543 GMT 0000 GMT (Dow Jones) The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's decision to decline to offer Swiss franc repos in recent days suggests it is unwilling to accept lower franc money market rates, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "We attribute the latest decline in franc interest rates and negative rates on the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward market to the excess liquidity in the local money market rather than anything else," says economist Reto Huenerwadel. "It's a fact, however, that over the last days the monetary conditions in Switzerland continued to loosen," he adds. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0543 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY NDFs offshore extend gains on USD general strength vs major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; 1-year USD/CNY NDFs at 6.7560/6.7619 vs 6.7170/6.7220 late yesterday. Still, upside limited on lingering hopes China may allow CNY appreciation vs USD this year, says Hong Kong-based trader at Asian bank. "My personal feeling is that the one-year NDFs will be very toppish around 6.7700," trader says. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com) [ 05-25-10 0229ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100525e65p0004p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
