\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 890 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 2010\par 07:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: RBA GOVERNOR SAYS RATES "PRETTY CLOSE" TO AVERAGE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia Governor Glenn Stevens on Friday hinted at a coming pause in interest rate hikes, saying interest rates for borrowers and depositors are now "pretty close" to their average of the past 10-12 years. (published 0508 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: JAPAN OPPOSES PRESSURING CHINA ON YUAN BEFORE G-20 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor on Thursday signaled their opposition to openly pressuring China to revalue its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the yuan, seemingly distancing themselves from the U.S. and European positions ahead of key international meetings. (published 0224 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER SAYS G-7 OFFICIALS FOCUSED ON GREECE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Top Group of Seven finance officials Thursday devoted a "significant" part of their dinner conversation to Greece's debt turmoil, Japan's finance minister said, underscoring how concerned they are about a European problem that continues to roil global financial markets. (published 0554 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA SWAN: SHALLOW DOWNTURN MEANS RECOVERY FROM POSITION OF STRENGTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The shallow downturn in Australia means the economy is recovering from a position of strength relative to other developed nations, Treasurer Wayne Swan said at an investor briefing in New York Thursday. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA COMMERCE MINISTRY: 2010 RETAIL SALES TO GROW AROUND 16% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's retail sales are likely to grow around 16% this year, partly due to rising inflationary pressures amid the economy's recovery, the Ministry of Commerce said Friday. (published 0130 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN BANK MINISTER CALLS FOR LOOSE SUPERVISION AS GOLDMAN PROBE CONTINUES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's banking minister Friday called for a hands-off approach to financial supervision in Japan, speaking even as authorities across the Pacific proceeded with their fraud probe against \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (published 0247 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: THAI CAPITAL ON EDGE AFTER DEADLY GRENADE BLASTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thai riot police faced down thousands of anti-government protesters Friday after grenade attacks left one woman dead and scores wounded in the latest bloodshed of the escalating political crisis. (published 0402 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN IMPORT PRICE INDEX +0.3% IN 1Q, EXPORT INDEX +3.8% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A price index of Australian imports rose 0.3% in the first quarter of 2010 from the fourth quarter 2009, the Australian Bureau of Statistics said Friday. (published 0132 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: NZ MIGRANT INFLOWS REMAIN STRONG, HELP SUSTAIN GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Migrant inflows into New Zealand surged nearly three-fold in the twelve months ended March, helping sustain growth in the nation as new comers boost retail spending and housing services. (published 0243 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NZ'S MARCH CREDIT CARD BILLINGS +1.2% VS FEB, +6.3% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's total credit card billings rose in March from February, data issued Friday by the Reserve \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  showed. (published 0307 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: SINGAPORE MARCH CPI UP 1.6% ON HIGHER FOOD, TRANSPORT COSTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's consumer prices in March rose due to higher food and transport costs, though the pace of increase was marginally below analyst expectations. (published 0500 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SINGAPORE 1Q PRIVATE HOME PRICES UP 5.6% VS 5.1% INITIAL ESTIMATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's government said Friday that private residential property prices rose 5.6% in the first quarter from the previous three months. (published 0457 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA TO GIVE SMALLER BUILDERS KRW3 TLN FOR UNSOLD APARTMENTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean government will give KRW3 trillion to buy some 20,000 unsold apartments to help smaller builders fight a shortage of liquidity. (published 0147 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: VIETNAM APRIL CPI +9.23% ON YEAR ON HIGHER TRANSPORT COSTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vietnam's consumer price index in April rose 9.23% from a year earlier, led by higher transport, housing and building materials costs and prices of foodstuff, the General Statistics Office said Friday. (published 0528 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: GLORIOUS PROPERTY POSTPONES BOND OFFERING - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese property developer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glorious Property Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . has put a global bond offering on hold as it awaits better market conditions. (published 0507 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TITAN PETROCHEMICALS MAKES NEW OFFER TO EXCHANGE US DOLLAR BOND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil trader and shipper \f28 \uc2 \f2 Titan Petrochemicals Group Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Thursday it plans to make a new offer worth US$254.2 million in total, comprising U.S. dollar senior notes, convertible bonds and a cash payment, to buy back its outstanding US$315.4 million worth of U.S. dollar senior notes due 2012. (published 0034 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    ==