\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Thai Shares Down 1.3% At Midday; May Fall Further\b0\par\par\uc2 1,736 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 2010\par 07:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0555 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares down 1.3% at 751.60 at midday break on across-the-board selling in the aftermath of grenade attacks in Bangkok's main financial hub last night. While index off morning session low of 746.59, selling pressure will likely remain. "No one knows what is going to happen during the weekend. Many people think tensions are mounting from the growing opposition against the red-shirt (anti-government) protesters so the chance of a standoff is growing. At that point, the government may not be able to control the situation, so investors are cashing in to mitigate risk," says Far East Securities analyst Weerachai Krongsamsri; adds further declines likely. Support is tipped at 740, with resistance at 755. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) was down 1.6% at THB252, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) declined 3.2% to THB83, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) lost 1.0% to TB146.50, \uc2 Indorama Ventures\uc2  (IVL.TH) dipped 1.2% to THB16, and \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) was down 2.7% at THB90.75. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0555 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea's recent appointment of some key government-friendly economic officials viewed as advocates of weaker KRW for export competitiveness has players expecting more forceful government presence in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb KRW gains, but local economic fundamentals suggest concerns may be misplaced. Several market participants say KRW strength due to fundamentals authorities can hardly fight; expected CNY appreciation could pull regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher; also fund inflows to local securities' markets likely to keep KRW demand solid. Moreover, former Vice Finance Minister Choi Joong-kyung, named president's economic adviser in late March, may return to office a chastened man; "he aggressively pushed for a weaker won and faced strong headwinds in the market," a BOK official said; "He may have changed now. I don't think he'll go against the market." See: =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Will \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  Take Bolder Steps On \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0550 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +0.3% at 8004.89, reclaiming psychological 8000-point mark on stronger-than-expected earnings reports by tech firms, decline in March jobless rate, says Allen Lin, manager at Concord Securities. Tips main index to consolidate with upward bias in 7900-8100 range next week as 1Q results, 2Q guidance all due by end-April, in 7950-8050 band Monday. "The Taiex may rise further next week but a big jump is unlikely as rosy earnings probably have been priced in," he says. Electronics up 0.6% with \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) +3.6% at NT$37.65, \uc2 Largan Precision\uc2  (3008.TW) +3.2% at NT$514.00. (crystal.hsu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0544 GMT [Dow Jones] AmResearch tips Malaysia's economy to grow 8% this year, fastest since 1996 (vs 4.5%-5.5% official forecast) on broad-based recovery due to strengthening domestic, external demand. House also says GDP may rise at 9.8% on-year in 1Q (highest in a decade), led by private sector spending by households, exports. Economist Manokaran Mottain says 2010 growth drivers will be manufacturing sector (+12.3%), led by electrical, electronics sub-sector, services sector (+7.1%); private consumption will rise (+4.5% vs +0.8% in 2009) on better jobs market, higher disposable incomes, consumer confidence. Exports likely +15%, higher current account surplus of MYR125 billion eyed. Headline inflation expected +2.5%; "given the assumption of stronger economic momentum and higher inflation rates, we reckon year-end target for OPR will be at 3% (vs 2.75% previously) now," says Manokaran. OPR at 2.25% now. Sees USD/MYR at 3.1000 by year-end. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0534 GMT [Dow Jones] CMC says Australian shares are lower on profit taking as risk aversion creeps into the market. "This risk aversion is not really coming from the US, rather it stems from the ongoing sovereign debt saga in Greece and technical factors pointing to further downward progression for the benchmark index," CMC dealer Ben Le Brun says. Adds the market dragged down by lower commodity prices and the one-off event of CSL's (CSL.AU) slump. "The market is definitely finding it difficult moving into top gear, but investors appear comfortable enough in third given the current risks to the market." Says share prices to be driven by earnings from this point and commentary from companies is sounding cautious, pointing to slightly more constrained earnings than previously thought. S&P/ASX 200 down 0.5% to 4883.1 half an hour out from close. (alex.wilson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0525 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore March CPI +1.6% on-year, +0.4% adjusted on-month, in line with median 1.7% rise forecast by 7 economists surveyed by Dow Jones; results unlikely to trigger any new tightening moves, says David Cohen of Action Economics. "The picture remains of a gradually firming pricing outlook but won't upset anyone. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has always prided itself in being ahead of the curve," Cohen says; adds need to watch housing prices closely. March housing prices down 0.7% on-year, but "market's been firming up," he says. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0519 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares ended tad higher, lifted by foreign funds that accumulated clutch of property stocks, conglomerates; benchmark PSEi closed 0.2% higher to 3244.45 but down 0.6% on week; volume heavy; gainers led by \uc2 Ayala Corp\uc2 . (AC.PH), up 3.7% to PHP347.50, Metro Pacific Investments (MPI.PH), up 3.4% to PHP3, SM Prime Holdings (SMPH.PH), up 1.0% to PHP10, and \uc2 Vista Land & Lifescapes\uc2  (VLL.PH), up 1.1% to PHP1.90. Jomar Lacson, research head at Campos, Lanuza & Co., says market should consolidate in coming days, with investors likely to lock-in profit ahead of the May 10 elections; "I would advise investors lessen their exposure leading up to the elections just to account for any uncertainty." Market expected to move within 3200-3300 band. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0516 GMT [Dow Jones] Topix real estate subindex up 1.1%, best performer on board, as investors moving away from exporters (especially due to weak euro) and picking up domestic-demand shares, market observers say. "Manufacturers' earnings recovery is already priced in, but real estate stocks still have room to rise mid-term," fund manager at Japanese asset management firm says. Recovery in commercial real estate market (such as office building rents) as well as accessibility of real estate firms to lending vehicles will likely continue. Major developers announce earnings from April 30; if companies announce conservative outlooks for this FY through March and share prices drop, it may be buying opportunity, market observers say. \uc2 Sumitomo Realty & Development\uc2  (8830.TO) up 1.1% at Y1,860, \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  (8801.TO) up 1.1% at Y1,629; \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  has targets of Y2,200 on former, Y2,000 on latter. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0506 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW trims gains on exporter sales, while "those who had grabbed dollar longs, taking their cue from a sharply lower euro, now seem to be putting in some stop-loss sell orders," boosting KRW, says local bank trader. Notes unusual, weak correlation between EUR and KRW lately. USD/KRW now nearly flat at 1,108.5 vs 1,108.3 late yesterday in Seoul but off earlier 1,111.7 high. Traders put key support near 1,107.1--lowest intraday level marked so far for this year. Note, traders suspect BOK bought USD near KRW1,107 yesterday. "They (BOK) may kick in if the dollar falls by two or three won," from here, adds trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0500 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.8% at 21,288.90 midday, tracking falls in China markets (Shanghai Composite down 0.3%, Shenzhen Composite down 0.3%) with property stocks leading losses on concerns of more tightening measures. Among HK developers, Cheung Kong (0001.HK) down 0.8% at HK$98.15, \uc2 Sun Hung Kai Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) down 1.5% at HK$111.60; among China developers, China Overseas (0688.HK) down 2.8% at HK$14.70, \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) down 2.5% at HK$14.26. Jamie Coutts at \uc2 BGC Partners\uc2  says index likely to break its latest trading range (in 21,206-22,388 range so far this month) by end-May, suggests re-entry level when HSI tests 20,500-21,000. "I'm a bit biased for a downside break (on HSI in the near-term), given the recent bearish moves from the Shanghai shares market." Volume modest at HK$34.55 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0500 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore March CPI rose marginally slower than expected on-year, with price pressures coming from food, transport costs. March CPI +1.6% on year vs +1.7% poll forecast; +0.4% on-month adjusted, in line with poll. While data show price pressures in Singapore rising, increases still coming off very sharp falls in 2009. Reading unlikely to ring alarm bells at MAS just yet but case appears to be slowly mounting for possibly further tightening at October meeting. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0500 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi still down 0.3% at 1734.88 in light volume led by profit-taking in shipyards, program selling weighing on some large caps; Daewoo Shipbuilding (042660.SE) down 2.7% at KRW21,800, bellwether \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 1.4% at KRW829,000, KB Financial (105560.SE) down 0.7% at KRW54,900. But Kospi's fall limited as foreign buying continues; likely to drift near current level rest of session. LG Display (034220.SE) +3.3% at KRW45,400, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) +4.0% at KRW130,000 after reporting earnings surprise, upbeat outlook yesterday, say analysts. \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) +0.6% at KRW26,550, off early low of KRW25,950; reports solid 1Q earnings, bright outlook. (soo-kyung.seo@dowjones.com) [ 04-23-10 0155ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100423e64n0003r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
