\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,227 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2010\par 10:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK JAN CLAIMANT COUNT POSTS SURPRISE RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. unemployment rose sharply in January after two months of declines, while average earnings rose at a record-low pace for a third straight month, data showed Wednesday. (published 0937 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOE: DECISION TO PAUSE EASING FINELY BALANCED FOR SOME MEMBERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee was unanimous in its decision to suspend its bond-buying program in February, but for some members it was a close call, meeting minutes released Wednesday showed. (published 0939 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPAIN PM: GDP TO RETURN TO GROWTH IN 1H, ABLE TO CUT DEFICIT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spain's gross domestic product will return to growth during the first half of 2010, Prime Minister Jose Luis Rodriguez Zapatero said Wednesday, after final data showed that the country was still in recession in the fourth quarter of 2009. (published 0856 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB QUADEN: NO CHANGE IN INTEREST RATES PLANNED FOR NOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't change interest rates for the time being, as it doesn't see imminent risks of inflation, ECB Governing Council member Guy Quaden said Wednesday. (published 0500 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL BANKS MAY NEED $221B EXTRA CAPITAL UNDER NEW RULES- JPM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Banks could be forced to come up with $221 billion in additional capital to meet regulatory reform proposals, and their returns on equity could be slashed by more than half from current levels, analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said in a research note Wednesday. (published 0932 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN PRIME MINISTER: NO CHANGE IN STANCE TO KEEP SALES TAX RATE FOR 4 YEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan Prime Minister Yukio Hatoyama said Wednesday that his government still intends to keep the sales tax rate at 5% for at least four years, as per the ruling party's election manifesto. (published 0713 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par LONG-TERM JGBS DOWN; FADING GREECE DEBT WOE LIFTS TOKYO STOCK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Long-term Japanese government bonds were lower Wednesday as a sharp upturn in Tokyo shares accelerated the selling of safe-haven assets amid receding concern over European debt. (published 0732 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPAIN FINAL 4Q GDP AT -0.1% ON QUARTER, -3.1% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spain continued to lag the euro-zone economic recovery in the fourth quarter, but the rate of decline in output eased, final data from Spain's National Statistics Institute, or INE, showed Wednesday. (published 0800 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN BUSINESS OUTLOOK UP AMID EXPORTS, INVESTMENT - DIHK POLL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the first time since the financial crisis started, more German companies are optimistic than pessimistic, thanks to higher exports and domestic demand for investment goods, according to a survey by the German Chambers of Commerce, or DIHK. (published 0930 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GREEK MERCHANTS GROUP CALLS FOR MEASURES TO BOOST MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greece urgently needs measures to boost a slumping retail market and restore consumer confidence, a Greek merchants association said Wednesday, in a veiled critique of the government's efforts to fix the country's public finances. (published 0839 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TURKEY KEEPS RATES ON HOLD AS RECOVERY LOOMS LARGER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Turkish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held its key overnight borrowing rate steady at 6.5% for a third straight month on Tuesday amid a glut of positive economic news and stellar commercial banking profits. (published 0747 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TURKEY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SELLS TRY2 BILLION AT 6.77% IN REPO -IHA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Turkish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday boosted market liquidity by selling 2 billion Turkish lira in a repurchase-agreement auction, the Ihlas News Agency, or IHA, reported. (published 0923 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S ECONOMIC REBOUND STRONGEST SINCE 1970S -SURVEY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's economy is showing every sign of a full blooded upswing, with activity recovering at its fastest pace since the volatile period of the mid-1970s, according to data released Wednesday. (published 0331 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SINGAPORE NON-OIL EXPORTS WEAKER THAN EXPECTED IN JANUARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's key exports grew slower than expected in January mostly due to a very weak pharmaceuticals sector although resilience in electronics exports suggests the island-state's trade recovery is likely to continue in 2010. (published 0614 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BANK INDONESIA: 4TH QUARTER BALANCE OF PAYMENTS SURPLUS UP TO $4 BLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's balance of payments surplus rose to $4 billion in the fourth quarter of 2009 from $3.5 billion in the July-September period, fueled by strong demand for commodity products, Bank Indonesia said Wednesday. (published 0529 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PHILIPPINES JANUARY BALANCE OF PAYMENTS AT $1.23 BLN SURPLUS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippines had a balance of payments surplus of $1.23 billion in January this year, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data released Wednesday show. (published 0649 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOK GOVERNOR: DECISION TO HIKE RATE 'NOT TOO DISTANT' \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Korea Governor Lee Seongtae said Wednesday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will start raising its policy interest rate as soon as businesses show signs of recovery, adding that a rate hike could come in the near future. (published 0926 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 SOUTH KOREA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WON UP LATE; BANK OF KOREA LIKELY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENED\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AGAIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean won rose sharply late Wednesday, likely prompting a second straight day of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of Korea to prop the dollar. (published 0805 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SOUTH KOREA BANKS' OVERSEAS BORROWING CONDITIONS STABLE - REGULATOR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean banks' foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  borrowing conditions have remained stable despite recent concerns about debt problems in Europe, the Financial Supervisory Service said Wednesday. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WHITE HOUSE TO UNVEIL $1.5 BLN IN TRANSPORTATION GRANTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The White House, marking the $787 billion economic stimulus package's one-year anniversary, on Wednesday will unveil $1.5 billion in grants for transportation projects, senior administration officials said. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US TREASURY REPORTS UPTURN IN LOANS BY TAXPAYER-AIDED BANKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New loan originations rose 13% in December while outstanding loan balances fell 1% from the prior month, according to a survey of bank lending issued by the U.S. Treasury Department. (published 2300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: THE POUND'S NEXT LEG DOWN IS ON ITS WAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling bears are just waiting for the next foot to fall. (published 0815 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MONEY TALKS: CHINA'S TSY SALES DON'T MEAN A MAJOR DOLLAR SHIFT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China may be back in second spot among the largest foreign holders of U.S. Treasurys, but it's not moving aggressively to shift away from U.S. dollar assets. (published 0020 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ(2/17) HEARD ON THE ST: THE SOVEREIGN-DEBT CHALLENGE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Western governments will be hoping they don't -- but there are some signs it is happening already. (published 0323 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: BULK OF US STIMULUS SPENDING YET TO COME \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Obama administration's economic-stimulus program has delivered about a third of its total $787 billion budget during its first year, much of that to maintain social services and government jobs and to provide tax cuts for workers. Now, the pace and direction of stimulus spending are about to change. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HK NOVEMBER-JANUARY JOBLESS RATE LIKELY EDGED LOWER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's unemployment rate likely continued to edge lower for the fifth consecutive period in the three months ended Jan. 31, as domestic consumption and exports improved--albeit gradually. (published 0256 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FINANCIAL CRISIS FORCES 17 MILLION ASIANS INTO DEEP POVERTY - ADB/UN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MANILA --Seventeen million Asians have fallen into extreme poverty due to the global financial crisis, the Asian Development Bank and the United Nations said on Wednesday. (published 0712 GMT) [ 17-02-10 0957GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100217e62h000oq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pi