\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 South Korea \highlight\uc2  Won Up Late; Bank Of Korea Likely  \highlight22\uc2 Intervened \highlight\uc2  Again\b0\par\par\uc2 369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2010\par 09:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     Close    Change \par  USD/KRW           1,142.20  -9.30 \par  JPY/KRW            12.6489  -0.1712 \par  3-Year Treasuries  4.10%    -2 bps \par  5-Year Treasuries  4.73%    unchanged \par  10-Year Treasuries 5.30%    unchanged \par  20-Year Treasuries 5.46%    unchanged \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won rose sharply late Wednesday, likely prompting a second straight day of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of Korea to prop the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market during the session to slow the won's rise by purchasing small batches of dollars, three Seoul-based traders said.\par \par \uc2 They may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  largely through one foreign bank and one local bank, buying dollars around the KRW1,141-KRW1,143 levels, they added. One of those traders estimated the purchases to total between $500 million and $600 million.\par \par \uc2 The suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen supporting the dollar near KRW1,150 Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "It's not about keeping the dollar at a certain level since they can't reverse the direction of the dollar's move," said a foreign bank trader. He forecast the dollar to trade in a KRW1,130-KRW1,150 range Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The won gained 0.8% on a broad return of risk appetite, tracking overnight gains in the euro and robust regional stocks during the Asian hours.\par \par \uc2 Most domestic treasury bonds ended flat after a listless session. March bond futures ended three ticks higher at 110.40 after moving in a tight 110.29-110.40 range.\par \par \uc2 "Bonds are just taking a breather. There wasn't a lot of trading and there weren't any big moves in futures either," said a local securities firm trader. "It might take a while, but the three-year yield is likely to fall to as low as 4.00% eventually, given weak prospects for a domestic rate hike."\par \par \uc2 He expects the yield curve to move aimlessly, with buying interest likely shifting back and forth between shorter and longer-end bonds.\par \par \uc2 -By Min-Jeong Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-3700-1908; min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com [ 17-02-10 0805GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1063 1858   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100217e62h000hw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
