\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/MYR Tad Lower Late; 3.3800-3.4000 Range Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,288 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2009\par 10:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR tad lower late at 3.3925 vs Monday's 3.3950; dealer says pair generally tracking USD/SGD but moving in tight range for most day, "stuck" around 3.3890-3.3950 levels. "There was some ringgit selling in the morning and we had expected the pair to test 3.4000, but the MYR has held up well... with a lot of corporate sellers (of USD) lining up at the 3.3950-3.3980 level," dealer says. Adds, some players could be selling USD, hedging for an expected stronger MYR next year. Still, tips pair to stay roughly within range Wednesday at 3.3800-3.4000. (KPL)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0911 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling gets a marginal lift from the 1.4% month on month rise in UK house price reported by the Halifax. The market had been looking for a 0.5% increase so the reaction should have been a little better, however \uc2 Moody's\uc2  warning on the UK's AAA rating and a disappointing overnight BRC retail sales report have taken the wind out of sterling's sail. GBP/USD trades at 1.6365, from the day's high of 1.6474. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0905 GMT [Dow Jones] The DMO taps GBP3.75 billion UKT 3.75% September 2019 Tuesday taking the total outstanding issue size to GBP20 billion. With the ten-year bond at historically cheap levels against both the short and long ends of the gilt curve, there was significant switching into the ten-year Monday. Plus, with the BOE buying GBP980 million of the UKT 4.75% 2020 and UKT 4.25% 2032 as well as Tuesday afternoon's GBP1.7 billion three- to ten-year buyback, the auction is likely to go well, says \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  strategists. Although not historically cheap, the large outstanding issue size could make the bond the ten-year benchmark for the swaps market. (NKA)\par \par \uc2 0903 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar is showing no clear direction in European hours, falling against the euro despite Bernanke's reassertion in US hours Monday that US rates are set to stay low for the long haul. The euro now trades at $1.4814. The dollar has also fallen against the yen, trading now at Y89.08, with Japanese exporters once again seen as keen sellers. However, it has jumped against the pound, which has been stung by a fresh reminder that the UK needs to get deficits under control in order to hold onto AAA ratings. GBP trades at $1.6372. (KJM)\par \par \uc2 0902 GMT [Dow Jones] Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rise 2.1% on month in November, boosted by increase in value of non-dollar holdings and continued official purchases of dollars in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets; pace of growth expected to slow ahead as signs of dollar stability emerge and governments \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less. All countries but Malaysia reported reserves well above year-earlier levels; biggest monthly increases seen in places where central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily, such as Hong Kong, Thailand and \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 . With \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  estimating that 10 key emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including South Korean won and Chinese yuan, remain significantly undervalued vs dollar, reserves in most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  can still gain as valuations are corrected. See =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Asia Reserves Up 2.1% In Nov; Dollar Outlook Is Key LINK: system=djm;xrf="SRW06963" (MSA)\par \par \uc2 0858 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD's break below its 9 month uptrend at 1.4897 and then the mid-November low of 1.4801 confirms a top is in place at 1.5145 says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Axel Rudolph. He now looks for slippage toward 1.4675 and 1.4625 and if the latter gives way, 1.4480. EUR/USD now at 1.4815. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0852 GMT [Dow Jones] USD-funded carry trade has legs, even as recent data lead some to talk about when Fed may raise rates; for greenback, economic fundamentals, rate differentials each play a role, and in both cases there's no screaming reason to think USD's appeal as funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will disappear. Investors have been borrowing in low-yielding USD to put into higher-yielding assets, whether as pure \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade into likes of AUD, or foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated debt or stocks. With U.S. rates at rock-bottom, country's economy lagging, such trades have been gaining appeal with Australia raising rates, other central banks in Asia starting to inch towards tightening, but Friday's surprisingly strong U.S. payrolls, coupled with oversold signals on USD, have some talking about potential for a more prolonged USD rebound in coming weeks and months, with carry trades unwound. Not so fast. See =MONEY TALKS: Don't Get Carried Away On The US Dollar. (RXM)\par \par \uc2 0851 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  expects Greek government bonds and other "spread products" to underperform German bunds at least until mid-January, and an overweight position should therefore be kept in bunds and French OATs within the euro zone government bond market, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  strategist David Schnautz. Adds that Monday's very successful six-month German bubill auction supports \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 's view that core government bonds with the euro zone, and German government securities in particular, will see healthy demand. (BEM)\par \par \uc2 0850 GMT [Dow Jones] The ruble is now the worst-performing emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a monthly basis, having fallen by 4.6%, notes \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 . This is odd, given that oil has been stable of late. A year-end fiscal spending spree, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and firmer USD have all put RUB under pressure, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says. Still, this could offer a nice buying opportunity and \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says it's looking to add USD/RUB shorts at the start of next year. USD/RUB trades at 30.18. (KJM)\par \par \uc2 0847 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japan Series 12 almost flat at 136 bps vs yesterday's 137 due to lack of fresh trading cues. As JPY rise, stock weakness -- main movers of Japan credit market in a past few weeks -- seem to near end, CDS spreads also gradually narrowing, though at very slow pace; some traders expect benchmark CDS index could fall to as low as 130 bps this week. Single-name CDS deals include \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2 . (6758.TO) at 60 bps vs 70 bps on Nov. 26, \uc2 Nippon Steel Corp\uc2 . (5401.TO) at 55 bps vs 60 bps on Nov. 27, \uc2 Nikon Corp\uc2 . (7731.TO) at 50 bps, \uc2 Hitachi Ltd\uc2 . (6501.TO) at 95 bps vs 94 bps on Dec. 3, \uc2 Mitsui O.S.K. Lines Ltd\uc2 . (9104.TO) at 125 bps vs 90 bps on May 22. (MEF)\par \par \uc2 0843 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds steady late, yields stuck in 1-2 bps range dealer says. "The redemption of the June 2012 didn't move the market as the amount was not significant." Bank Negara said earlier today plans to redeem MYR500 million worth of callable bond at par on December 15. Tender size of re-opening of Feb 2012 MGS at MYR2.5 billion "within market expectations". Yields for February 2015 MGS down 2 bp at 3.76%, November 2019s +1 bp at 4.25%, May 2013s down 1 bp at 3.23%. (KPL)\par \par \uc2 0842 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS flat or lower, consolidating after yesterday's sharp gains. 1-year unchanged at 2.05%-2.11%, 5-year down 1 bp at 3.55%-3.64%. "I think the swaps will extend gains in the coming sessions as the macroeconomic data to be released soon will likely send out positive signals," says Beijing-based local bank trader. (WYL) [ 12-08-09 0412ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091208e5c800064\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
