\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 807 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2009\par 13:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIA CENTRAL BANKS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AGAIN AS DOLLAR TUMBLES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar continued to tumble against most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday, prompting another wave of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by central banks in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Taiwan, and the Philippines seeking to limit damage to their export industries. (published 0343 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP:GERMAN AUG INDUS OUTPUT UP, AMID HIGH CONSTRUCTION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German industrial production rose 1.7% on the month in August, roughly in line with expectations, driven by a strong construction sector, data from the Economics Ministry showed Thursday. (published 1000 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA JOBS SURGE BOOSTS RBA HIKE EXPECTATIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The number of employed Australians surged in September, defying economists' expectations of a fall and firming up expectations of more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  interest rate increases this year. (published 0237 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN AUG CURR ACCT SURPLUS UP, BUT LOW RATES DRAG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's current account surplus rose slightly less than expected in August, as low interest rates overseas and the strong yen ate into Japanese firms' investment earnings, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Thursday. (published 0213 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: FRENCH AUG TRADE GAP WIDENS TO EUR3.41B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 France's trade deficit widened more than expected in August to EUR3.41 billion from a revised EUR1.02 billion deficit in July, due to a drop in exports, according to the latest seasonally adjusted customs data released Thursday. (published 0700 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN ECON ADVISERS' COUNCIL: NO ROOM FOR MAJOR TAX CUTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's new government doesn't have the room to make major tax cuts, the standing Council of Economic Advisers, or Sachverstaendigenrat, said in a statement Thursday. (published 1033 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN COALITION PARTIES WANT BIGGER BUBA ROLE IN BK OVERSIGHT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's center-right parties have agreed to give the Deutsche Bundesbank a significantly enhanced role for banking supervision, at the expense of financial regulator BaFin. (published 0909 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SOUTH KOREA, EU TO SIGN FREE TRADE DEAL NEXT WEEK - MINISTRY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea and the European Union will sign a free trade deal next week after resolving the final sticking points, the trade ministry said Thursday. (published 0805 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN SEP CORP BANKRUPTCY CASES DOWN 15.7% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Data on Japanese corporate bankruptcies in September from private credit research agency Teikoku Databank Ltd. (published 0430 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN SEPTEMBER MACHINE TOOL ORDERS TABLE OF DATA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Preliminary data on machine tool orders for September from the Japan Machine Tool Builders' Association. (published 0615 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA FIN SECY:CAN'T CONTROL RUPEE RISE IF MOVEMENT NOT JITTERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indian authorities can't do much to control the rupee's rise against the U.S. dollar as long as the movement isn't volatile, the federal finance secretary said Thursday. (published 1013 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA PANEL HEAD: NEED TO CONTINUE WITH EASY MONETARY POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the government should continue with their easy policy stance until economic growth recovers to 7%, the head of the country's top government think-tank said Thursday. (published 0836 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA GOVT: WILL STICK TO EXPANSIONARY FISCAL POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean government will stick to its expansionary fiscal policy for the time being as private sector activity continues to be tepid and the strength of the global economy's recovery remains uncertain, the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Thursday. (published 0115 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    === DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY === \par  \par  \par =TAKING STOCK: THE RIGHT WAY TO REGULATE A BANK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At last, it seems, the political right is starting to come out fighting over the future of the banking system. (published 1013 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DOLLAR FALLS, AUSSIE SOARS ON JOBS DATA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Renewed optimism over the global recovery knocked the dollar lower in Europe. (published 0918 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: THE DIVERGING FORTUNES OF NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swedish krona and the Norwegian krone are once again parting ways. (published 0716 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: USD REMAINS BEARISH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: the pair has broken above its bullish flag upper boundary and remains well directed. Suggest long positions above 1.47 with targets at 1.4775 and 1.4825. The downside penetration of 1.47 will call for a slide towards 1.465 and 1.4625. (published 0635 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: PREPARING FOR RISES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The contracts are pulling back on their supports ahead of new up moves. (published 0744 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =OFF THE RUN: REPO MARKET ACTIVITY WON'T COME SURGING BACK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The crucial fixed-income securities lending market is recovering from the woes of the financial crisis, but it may never return to its former self. (published 1925 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =IN THE MONEY: MORE EVIDENCE OF SOME BAD TARP DECISIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Here's more evidence the Treasury Department wasn't always smart about deciding which banks got bailout funds. (published 2012 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Katrina Bishop, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 207 842 9376; katrina.bishop@dowjones.com [ 08-10-09 1100GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091008e5a8000mm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
