\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: IMF Urges BOK To Keep Rates Low Amid Asset Inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2009\par 07:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds economist's comment, background)\par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The International Monetary Fund said the Bank of Korea should keep its monetary policy loose, but that the Seoul authorities haven't ruled out rate hikes if general asset prices rise.\par \par \uc2 The IMF, in an annual assessment report on South Korea issued Thursday, said that after the BOK slashed rates by 325 basis points from October to February, "Monetary-policy accommodation should be maintained for now, while concerns about asset-price appreciation are best addressed through prudential regulation."\par \par \uc2 The Korean authorities, in a meeting that ended July 7, "agreed with the need for an accommodative monetary stance but remained concerned about its effect on asset prices," the IMF said. "The authorities did not rule out interest-rate hikes should more generalized asset-price rises become a concern."\par \par \uc2 Bank of Korea Governor Lee Seongtae said earlier this month that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next move, when it comes, is "clearly up," causing a massive sell-off in the bond market on fears that a rate increase could come earlier than expected.\par \par \uc2 Both the government and the BOK have said they would step up monitoring of the property market following a rise in mortgage lending and higher housing prices in recent months.\par \par \uc2 But many economists expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to switch to tightening some time in the first quarter of next year given as-yet tame inflation and uncertain economic growth prospects.\par \par \uc2 "Most indicators, including inflation, don't point to a rate increase any time soon. Asset price hikes in some areas alone won't be an excuse for any rate move," said Chun Chong-woo, head of macroeconomics and asset allocation department at \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In its latest report, the IMF also said: "While there are signs of excess liquidity, stock market valuations and house prices remain well in line with global peers and don't warrant an immediate monetary policy response."\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month the BOK left its benchmark interest rate steady at an all-time low of 2.00% for a sixth consecutive month.\par \par \uc2 Korea should also keep its fiscal stance expansionary in 2010 as the economic recovery is likely to be "drawn out," but the authorities also need to demonstrate a plan for fiscal consolidation in the medium term, the IMF said.\par \par \uc2 It said the Korean won is "considerably below most estimates of its long-term equilibrium level" as a result of capital outflows, but that this should be temporary.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  has "abstained completely" this year from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the IMF said, but if the authorities should step in, any "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market should remain confined to smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 At 0530 GMT Friday, the dollar fetched KRW1,253.3, up from its close Thursday of 1,246.9.\par \par \uc2 -By In-Soo Nam, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-2198-2234; In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com [ 21-08-09 0538GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090821e58l0004q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
