\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 900 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2009\par 13:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =DATA SNAP: OECD CONSUMER PRICES RECORD FIRST ANNUAL FALL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer prices in developed economies were lower in June than in the same month a year earlier, the first annual decline since records began in 1971. (published 1000 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EURO-ZONE JUN PPI ANNUAL FALL SUGGESTS DEFLATION RISK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone producer prices rose for the first time on the month since July 2008 in June, while the annual measure posted a fresh record drop highlighting that the risk of deflation remains very real, data from European Union statistics agency Eurostat showed Tuesday. (published 1002 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN GDP EXPECTED TO TURN POSITIVE IN APR-JUN - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's gross domestic product likely turned positive in the April-June period, the Mainichi Shimbun reported Tuesday, citing forecasts from 10 different think-tanks. (published 2238 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par YOSANO: NOT WORRIED ABOUT RECENT RISE IN L/T INTEREST RATES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance and Banking Minister Kaoru Yosano said Tuesday that he is not worried about the recent rise in long-term interest rates. (published 0254 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S&P REVISES CHINA PPTY SECTOR OUTLOOK TO NEUTRAL VS NEGATIVE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Tuesday it revised its short-term sector outlook on China's property developers to neutral from negative due to their improved liquidity in the first half of this year. (published 0444 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN JULY CAR SALES UP AROUND 30% ON YEAR - SOURCES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New car sales in Germany in July rose about 30% on the year boosted by the scrapping incentive, two people familiar with the matter said Tuesday. (published 0803 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =SPAIN JULY JOBLESS CLAIMS -0.6% ON MO, +46% ON YR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spain's jobless claims shrunk for the third consecutive month in July as the summer tourism season and the government's EUR8 billion infrastructure plan provided some relief to the country's ailing job market. (published 0820 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =AUSTRALIAN ECONOMY PULLS CLEAR OF RECESSION THREAT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian retail sales weakened unexpectedly in June as aggressive department store price discounting likely eroded the profits of shop owners. (published 0226 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =RBA SHIFTS MONETARY POLICY SETTINGS TO NEUTRAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  Tuesday indicated that it is no longer considering a further reduction of interest rates and is instead adopting a neutral policy bias due to a stronger-than-expected economy. (published 0559 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par RBI SUBBARAO: INDIA REFORMS WILL NOT SLOW DESPITE ECON CRISIS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India won't slow down on reforms, particularly in the financial sector, despite the global economic crisis, Reserve Bank of India Governor Duvvuri Subbarao said Tuesday. (published 1003 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par =WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : PROFIT-TAKING LIFTS DLR, YEN; HITS HI-YIELDERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A reassessment of recent optimism about the global economy helped the dollar higher in Europe Tuesday. (published 0910 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =WSJ:CHINA MINISTRY: MOST RURAL MIGRANT WORKERS HAVE FOUND JOBS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The effects of China's stimulus efforts are filtering into the job market, with the government reporting that most rural migrant workers have now found new jobs after the mass layoffs of late last year. (published 0812 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: KEEPING A CAP ON RISK APPETITE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Global risk appetite is strong and could well get stronger this week. (published 0715 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: USD UNDER MORE PRESSURE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: the RSI lacks upward momentum; the pair should pull back on its new support ahead of a rebound. Suggest long positions above 1.432 with 1.445 and 1.4525 in sight. The downside breakout of 1.432 will open the way to 1.425 and 1.4205. (published 0721 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: UPWARD MOMENTUM LACKING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The contracts lack upward momentum and should face a weakness. (published 0730 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 SOUTH KOREA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  STEPS UP \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , BUT DANGERS LOOM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Authorities in Seoul are expected to continue their efforts to contain the South Korean won, but it's a strategy that may prove risky for a country that is heavily exposed to asset-price bubbles and rising energy costs. (published 0705 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW: BANK INDONESIA EXPECTED TO CUT KEY RATE 25 BPS WED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's nine-year low inflation rate in July will likely prompt the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cut its overnight policy rate another 25 basis points Wednesday, economists said. (published 0401 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: SMELLS LIKE CAPITULATION SPIRIT FOR THE US DLR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar's decline is starting to look suspiciously like capitulation selling, which could mean we're nearing a bottom. (published 0145 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: BETTING AGAINST THE HONG KONG PEG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong won't break its 26-year-old \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  peg to the U.S. dollar. Wanna bet? (published 0448 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =INTERVIEW: DPJ OFFICIAL: NO BOND SALES FOR SPENDING PROGRAMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Democratic Party of Japan could fund its planned Y7.1 trillion spending program for the next fiscal year without relying on borrowed money, a party official said, countering speculation that it would need to sell bonds to meet its spending goals. (published 0359 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(8/4) COLUMN: BERNANKE'S EXIT DILEMMA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke assured readers of this page that he has the tools to prevent the huge reserves he's pumped into the banks from generating an inflation that would abort an economic recovery. (published 0010 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Katrina Bishop, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 207 842 9376; katrina.bishop@dowjones.com [ 04-08-09 1100GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090804e584000o6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
