\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-S.Korean won hits 11-yr low as global woe lingers\b0\par\par\uc2 741 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2009\par 07:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Won falls to weakest since March 1998\par \par \uc2 * Worries about local dollar-funding shortages weigh\par \par \uc2 * Risk aversion seen barring quick turnaround (Adds more details, stocks)\par \par \uc2 By Yoo Choonsik and Cheon Jong-woo\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Feb 27 (Reuters) - The won fell to an 11-year low on Friday on investor concern about a shortage of U.S. dollars in South Korea and analysts said risk aversion fueled by the fragile global financial system would bar any quick turnaround.\par \par \uc2 Analysts conceded that some of the steps taken by the government of President Lee Myung-bak could help improve dollar funding in the long term but said that there was little the government could do to put an immediate halt to the selloff.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  late last year had helped the won <KRW=> recover some momentum, but troubles in eastern European economies this year have again sapped investors' confidence in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Investors are worried that the heightened risk aversion would make it extremely difficult for South Korea to tap into the global market to secure sufficient dollar funding to pay back maturing foreign debt.\par \par \uc2 "(The won is) just friendless at the moment, it's unwanted. But I think most of the bad news is already in the price. I don't think investors are taking sufficient encouragement and notice of the better numbers, such as on external debt," said Patrick Bennett, a strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "In an environment where risk appetite is low, you're not seeing the flow that would help lead a recovery in the won."\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> fell to as low as 1,542.9 per dollar, its weakest since March 1998, before recovering slightly to 1,534.8 by 0740 GMT on market talk of dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities in the afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Seoul shares <.KS11>, which gained as much 2.09 percent earlier in the session, fell back as the won fell, closing up 0.78 percent.\par \par \uc2 South Korea said on Thursday it would exempt foreign investors from income and capital gains taxes on investment in local treasury bonds and monetary stabilisation bonds to lure more foreign capital into the country.\par \par \uc2 HEAVY FOREIGN DEBT\par \par \uc2 The won's drop came even after data showed on Friday that South Korea's balance of payments surplus hit a near 2-year high in January as domestic investors sold off their foreign portfolio holdings and foreigners bought local shares. [ID:nSEO194004]\par \par \uc2 Banks in South Korea, including those run by foreigners, had $126.6 billion of foreign debt due this year as of the end of January, out of the total $182.3 billion worth, the government said Friday. [ID:nSEO212505]\par \par \uc2 South Korea has said its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of more than $200 billion and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap arrangements sealed with the United States, Japan and China totalling some $90 billion were more than sufficient to cover any emergency situation.\par \par \uc2 It has also said it was not obliged to repay all the debts because some were owed by foreign banks and some others were linked to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging contracts of their corporate clients.\par \par \uc2 The won came under more pressure in recent months as evidence mounts that the heavily export-dependent economy will be hit especially hard by the financial and economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's government expects the economy to shrink by about 2 percent this year but analysts forecast a contraction of as much as 7 percent, which would be the sharpest fall in the country's nearly 40 years of industrialisation.\par \par \uc2 The government has offered about $100 billion worth of fiscal spending and tax cut plans and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  slashed interest rates by 3.25 percentage points in less than five months to lift domestic demand and make up for collapsing exports.\par \par \uc2 NEWSLINKS:\par \par \uc2 * For details on South Korean foreign debts, double-click [ID:nSEO48526] and [ID:nSEO129537]\par \par \uc2 * For a graphic on won and South Korean CDS trends, see: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://customers.reuters.com/d/graphics/KR_WON0209.gif"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://customers.reuters.com/d/graphics/KR_WON0209.gif}}}\par \par \uc2 * For graphics on by-country breakdown of corporate bonds in Asia maturing 2009-2012 see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://customers.reuters.com/d/graphics/KR_CRPBND0209.gif"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://customers.reuters.com/d/graphics/KR_CRPBND0209.gif}}}\par \par \uc2 * For analysts' reaction, double-click [ID:nSEO124840] (Editing by Jonathan Hopfner & Kazunori Takada)\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL/KOREA (WRAPUP 2, PIX)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|G|RBN|RNP|PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090302e52r000xy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
